PlayableContent.put("file","cited",[["Multiplayer game design","#/map/t/multiplayer-design",[{"claim":"Elo’s expected score for player A against B is 1 / (1 + 10^((R_B - R_A)/400)), and the update after a game is R_A + K(S_A - E_A), where K sets how far a rating moves; typical K values in chess systems range from 10 to 40.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elo_rating_system"},{"claim":"Microsoft Research’s TrueSkill rates players by two numbers, a mean skill and an uncertainty, for matchmaking and ranking in multiplayer games including team games.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/trueskill-ranking-system/"}]],["Rhythm and music-timed play","#/map/t/rhythm-and-music-timed-design",[{"claim":"osu!standard hit windows: the 300 window is 80 minus 6 times OD ms, the 100 window 140 minus 8 times OD, the 50 window 200 minus 10 times OD (so plus or minus 50, 100 and 150 ms at OD 5).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://osu.ppy.sh/wiki/en/Beatmapping/Overall_difficulty"},{"claim":"Unity documents AudioSettings.dspTime as the audio system clock in seconds, counted from processed samples and paused with the game, and advises scheduling AudioSource.PlayScheduled 100 to 200 ms in advance.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/AudioSource.PlayScheduled.html"},{"claim":"Godot 4 documents syncing gameplay to audio as get_playback_position plus get_time_since_last_mix minus get_output_latency, and advises discarding a reading that decreases from the previous frame.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/audio/sync_with_audio.html"},{"claim":"Android documents the low-latency output feature flag as a continuous output latency of 45 ms or less; that NDK page (last updated 2024-01-03) says there is no API to query audio latency at runtime, but Oboe offers AudioStream::calculateLatencyMillis from stream timestamps, which misses delay the device does not know about (USB, HDMI, Bluetooth).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.android.com/ndk/guides/audio/audio-latency"},{"claim":"Beat Saber scores a cut out of 115 points: 70 for the approach swing, 30 for the follow-through and 15 for cutting near the centre.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://bsmg.wiki/ranking-guide.html"},{"claim":"Vice reports that Hi-Fi Rush attacks still happen off the beat but are more powerful in rhythm, and that the team added a fully optional beat meter.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://vice.com/en/article/88qyw5/how-a-designer-known-for-horror-made-the-musical-gaming-triumph-hi-fi-rush"}]],["Economy modelling and balance","#/map/t/economy-modelling-and-balance",[{"claim":"Matt Woodward's GDC 2017 talk on balancing the economy of Albion Online, a market-driven MMO, walks through balance anchors, core balance relationships and constraint-driven design using examples from that game.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://gdcvault.com/play/1024070"},{"claim":"Edward Castronova's 2023 GDC session, presented by Machinations.io, covers establishing currencies, distributing resources, trading, pricing and inflation, and using data and iterative testing to maintain an economy in live operation.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://gdcvault.com/play/1028982"},{"claim":"Ernest Adams and Joris Dormans, Game Mechanics: Advanced Game Design (New Riders, 2012), has chapters on the internal economy of a game and on Machinations, a tool for drawing, simulating and testing that economy.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.peachpit.com/store/adamsgame-mechanics-p1-9780321820273"},{"claim":"Edward Castronova's 2001 working paper Virtual Worlds: A First-Hand Account of Market and Society on the Cyberian Frontier estimated from EverQuest trades that Norrath's currency traded at about USD 0.0107 per unit and its nominal hourly wage was about USD 3.42, giving a GNP per capita between Russia and Bulgaria. It is a one-game estimate from 2001 (secondary reports of the figures); it does not show how to tune an economy.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.cesifo.org/en/publications/2001/working-paper/virtual-worlds-first-hand-account-market-and-society-cyberian"},{"claim":"CCP Games publishes a Monthly Economic Report for EVE Online. The February 2026 report (eveonline.com news) states that destruction value rose while mining and production value fell, that the velocity of ISK keeps decreasing, and that the mineral, ship and module price indices were down about 52, 21 and 13 percent over the past year. Raw data is downloadable. CCP has said in an EVE forum report thread (secondary to the article) that its sinks-and-faucets and money-supply plots differ because they use two data sources. This shows what a live economy measures and that public books get reconciled; it does not show that the reports improve balance.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.eveonline.com/news/view/monthly-economic-report-february-2026"}]],["Balance methods: curves, matrices and simulation","#/map/t/balance-methods",[{"claim":"Riot’s League of Legends champion balance framework watches four player groups (average, skilled, elite and professional). In its June 2020 update Riot widened the elite group from the top 0.1% to the top 0.5% of players to get enough games for win rates, and tightened the overpowered bands for average and skilled play by 0.5 points each.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.leagueoflegends.com/en-gb/news/dev/dev-balance-framework-update/"},{"claim":"Blizzard’s StarCraft II team describes using adjusted win percentages, which remove the matchmaker’s effect and factor in player skill, across millions of games, with roughly 55:45 treated as acceptable and ratios beyond 60:40 prompting investigation (from a 2010 article).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/1136961/starcraft-ii-the-balancing-act"},{"claim":"In a study presented in 2014 (posted to arXiv in 2019) on a shoot-em-up game, active learning with an upper-confidence-bound acquisition function needed about 70 playtest samples for its largest gain over random sampling, a level random sampling did not reach in the data set (138 players, 991 waves).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.01417"},{"claim":"Schreiber and Romero’s book \"Game Balance\" (CRC Press, 2021, 806 pages) covers transitive mechanics and cost curves, intransitive mechanics and payoff matrices, and the use of spreadsheets for balance.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.routledge.com/Game-Balance/Schreiber-Romero/p/book/9781498799577"},{"claim":"Blizzard’s role-queue announcement, published 18 July 2019, put a 2-2-2 role queue (two tanks, two damage, two support) with a separate skill rating per role into a beta season in patch 1.39 (13 August to 1 September), with full release on 1 September 2019.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://overwatch.blizzard.com/en-us/news/23060961/introducing-role-queue/"},{"claim":"Since Overwatch 2 launched in October 2022, standard play is 5v5 with one tank, two damage and two support; Blizzard has since run 6v6 tests.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://overwatch.blizzard.com/en-us/news/24104605/director-s-take-opening-up-the-conversation-on-5v5-and-6v6/"},{"claim":"Supercell’s Clash Royale team wrote on 13 February 2017 that it approaches card balance by combining playtesting with card use rates and win rates.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://supercell.com/en/games/clashroyale/blog/release-notes/balance-changes-coming-1"}]],["Power creep: balance as content keeps growing","#/map/t/power-creep-and-content-growth",[{"claim":"Hearthstone's Standard format was announced on 2 February 2016: decks built from cards released in the current and previous calendar year, plus the Basic and Classic sets, with Wild leaving every card legal. Blizzard listed a fresher meta, more freedom for designers and an easier start for new players as the goals.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://hearthstone.blizzard.com/en-us/news/19995505"},{"claim":"In 2021 Hearthstone replaced Basic and Classic in Standard with a Core Set of 235 cards, refreshed each year, and retired the Hall of Fame, moving its cards to Wild.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://hearthstone.blizzard.com/en-us/news/23620129/introducing-the-core-set-and-classic-format"},{"claim":"Wizards of the Coast announced in 2023 that Magic Standard sets would rotate every three years instead of two, starting with no rotation at the release of Wilds of Eldraine, to give cards more longevity.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://magic.wizards.com/en/news/announcements/revitalizing-standard"},{"claim":"In the Midnight pre-expansion notes, Blizzard describes a stat and item squish that reduces numbers across World of Warcraft for clarity and readability, and states that a player's power relative to enemies stays the same.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24244455/midnight-pre-expansion-content-update-notes"},{"claim":"Pokémon VGC play uses lettered regulation sets that restrict which Pokémon are legal, with each set able to last between one and three months and its contents announced up to 30 days ahead (the current handbook applies them to Pokémon Champions).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.pokemon.com/static-assets/content-assets/cms2/pdf/play-pokemon/rules/play-pokemon-vgc-tournament-handbook-en.pdf"},{"claim":"League of Legends physical damage after armour is physical damage x 100 / (100 + armour), so 100 armour halves it.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://wiki.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/Armor"}]],["Puzzles inside an action game","#/map/t/puzzles-in-action-spaces",[{"claim":"CrossCode, developed by Radical Fish Games, uses one ball-launching mechanic for both combat and puzzles; its puzzle elements include switches, boxes, barriers and fans, and the studio crowdfunded the game on Indiegogo in February 2015 before a PC release on 20 September 2018.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CrossCode"},{"claim":"The game-accessibility guideline \"Offer a means to bypass gameplay elements that aren’t part of the core mechanic, via settings or in-game skip option\" is rated Intermediate on Game Accessibility Guidelines, with examples including LA Noire’s skip action sequence option and Nier: Automata’s Auto Chips.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://gameaccessibilityguidelines.com/offer-a-means-to-bypass-gameplay-elements-that-arent-part-of-the-core-mechanic-via-settings-or-in-game-skip-option/"},{"claim":"New Super Mario Bros. Wii’s Super Guide appears after a player dies eight times in a row in a level in single-player and lets a computer-controlled Luigi demonstrate a safe route; the player can interrupt it or skip the level.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Super_Mario_Bros._Wii"},{"claim":"Portal was developed over roughly two years and four months at Valve by a team of no more than ten, grown from the 2005 DigiPen project Narbacular Drop, and released on 10 October 2007 in The Orange Box.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portal_(video_game)"},{"claim":"Breath of the Wild contains four Divine Beasts built as extended puzzles and shrines holding challenges from puzzles to battles (all but the four opening Great Plateau shrines are optional; those four must be completed to get the paraglider and leave the plateau); its producer said a physics-based puzzle can have several solutions.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Breath_of_the_Wild"}]],["Backtracking and return trips","#/map/t/backtracking-and-return-trips",[{"claim":"Hollow Knight: shop prices as listed by the community wiki: Cornifer sells area maps (30 Geo for Forgotten Crossroads up to 150 Geo for Queen’s Gardens and Fog Canyon; Iselda sells them dearer), the Quill costs 120 Geo, most pins 100 Geo (a few cost more) and the Wayward Compass charm 220 Geo. Maps are updated by resting at benches.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://hollowknight.wiki/w/Cornifer"},{"claim":"Outer Wilds: the time loop is 22 minutes and the only thing that persists is the ship-log data.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outer_Wilds"},{"claim":"Dark Souls: warping between bonfires comes with the Lordvessel, given after Ornstein and Smough in Anor Londo; you can warp from most bonfires but only to a small set of lit ones. Later games in the series differ.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://darksouls.wiki.fextralife.com/Lordvessel"},{"claim":"Elden Ring: the player can fast travel from the map to any discovered site of grace, except inside dungeons and in combat. Map fragments must be found to reveal the map.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://eldenring.wiki.gg/wiki/Mechanics"},{"claim":"Skyrim: fast travel is free from any outdoor location to any discovered location; carriages are the paid alternative.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://content1.m.uesp.net/wiki/Skyrim:Carriage"},{"claim":"Metroid Dread: Icon Highlight (select an icon, press Y) highlights similar points of interest across the map, can show all doors a newly gained ability opens, and areas with hidden items glow.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://metroid.nintendo.com/dread/news/metroid-dread-report-vol-9"}]],["Building a world players can read","#/map/t/worldbuilding-method",[{"claim":"Henry Jenkins's essay \"Game Design as Narrative Architecture\" (2004) argues that game designers are narrative architects, and describes four kinds of spatial story: evoked, enacted, embedded and emergent.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://web.mit.edu/~21fms/People/henry3/games&narrative.html"},{"claim":"Raph Koster, the lead designer of Ultima Online, writes that its creature ecology AI was switched off because of the cost of radial searches followed by pathfinding, and that the ecology's fixed resource pool collapsed for a separate reason: players hoarded crafted goods, so the pool ran dry.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.raphkoster.com/?p=46439"},{"claim":"Game Developer reports that a story bible, as described by a lead writer at Ubisoft Massive, begins as a two to three paragraph summary and is a living document across writing, design, art, sound and marketing.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/building-a-basic-story-bible-for-your-game"},{"claim":"Mobius Digital's Alex Beachum was scheduled to give two Outer Wilds talks at GDC 2020 (announced for March 2020; whether they were delivered or recorded was not confirmed), one on its level design as a world that changes over time and controls the pacing of narrative information, and one, with Loan Verneau, on curiosity-driven exploration from early 2D prototypes to the final mystery structure.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://gdconf.com/news/see-4d-level-design-outer-wilds-deconstructed-gdc-2020"},{"claim":"Game Informer's 2018 account of the making of Hollow Knight says Team Cherry found balancing explorer feel with a useful map the single biggest design challenge, solving it with a purchasable cartographer's map that fills in as the player explores; the Boneforest area was cut and Deepnest was reduced.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://gameinformer.com/2018/10/16/the-making-of-hollow-knight"}]],["Audio implementation","#/map/t/audio-implementation",[{"claim":"FMOD Studio has a free tier for small projects, and paid licences for larger revenue or budget. The thresholds and prices change, and could not be re-checked at the primary page on this date, so read the current terms before relying on them.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.fmod.com/legal"},{"claim":"Audiokinetic offers Wwise free for non-commercial and small projects within asset or budget limits, and sells commercial licences per project. The limits and prices change, and could not be re-checked at the primary page on this date, so read the current Audiokinetic pricing page.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.audiokinetic.com/pricing"},{"claim":"In Godot 4, AudioStreamPlayer has max_polyphony (default 1); calling play() once that many sounds are playing cuts off the oldest. AudioEffectCompressor has a sidechain property that names another bus to detect volume, which is how ducking is done.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_audioeffectcompressor.html"}]],["Pixel art direction","#/map/t/pixel-art-direction",[{"claim":"In Godot the stored defaults are filter mode Linear for canvas textures (default_texture_filter is 1), stretch mode disabled and scale mode fractional; the docs say projects created from Godot 4.7 start with stretch mode canvas_items, which is still not viewport. Pixel art needs Nearest, stretch mode viewport and scale mode integer (the integer mode and Window.content_scale_stretch exist from Godot 4.2).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/rendering/multiple_resolutions.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s snap_2d_transforms_to_pixel and snap_2d_vertices_to_pixel default to false, and the engine advises enabling only the transforms one rather than both.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_projectsettings.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s documentation recommends 640 by 360 as a baseline because it scales to 1280 by 720, 1920 by 1080, 2560 by 1440 and 3840 by 2160 with no black bars under integer scaling.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/rendering/multiple_resolutions.html"},{"claim":"Unity 6’s URP manual says the 2D Pixel Perfect package works only with the Built-In Render Pipeline. The URP Pixel Perfect Camera has Assets Pixels Per Unit, Reference Resolution, a Grid Snapping setting (None, Pixel Snapping or Upscale Render Texture) and a Crop Frame dropdown that includes Stretch Fill. The Built-In package says pixel snapping does not change Transform positions.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/urp/2d-pixelperfect-ref.html"},{"claim":"The NES shows up to 25 colours on screen from a 64-entry master palette and holds 64 sprites, 8 per scanline, of 8 by 8 or 8 by 16 pixels; PICO-8 is 128 by 128 pixels with 16 colours.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPU_palettes"}]],["Business model and monetisation","#/map/t/business-model",[{"claim":"On 11 December 2025 the US Ninth Circuit upheld the finding that Apple was in contempt of the Epic v. Apple injunction. On the US App Store, Apple may not block links to outside purchases or require them to be less prominent than its own purchase buttons, though it may stop them being more prominent. A total ban on commission for those purchases went too far: Apple may charge one limited to its genuine costs of handling the links, with the details sent back to the district court.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://cdn.ca9.uscourts.gov/datastore/opinions/2025/12/11/25-2935.pdf"},{"claim":"On 30 June 2026 the US Supreme Court agreed to hear Apple’s appeal of the contempt ruling, limited to the first question in Apple’s petition; Apple filed its brief on 14 September 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-1311.html"}]],["Launch and discoverability","#/map/t/launch-and-discoverability",[{"claim":"Steam’s content survey asks developers to disclose AI-generated content that ships with the game and reaches players (art, sound, narrative, localisation and any other content players see or hear), split into pre-generated content, made with AI tools during development, and live-generated content, made while the game runs. Efficiency gains from AI development tools are not covered. Valve rewrote the survey in January 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/contentsurvey"}]],["Soft launch and playable ads","#/map/t/soft-launch-and-playable-ads",[{"claim":"Playable ads are commonly built on MRAID (Mobile Rich Media Ad Interface Definitions), the IAB Tech Lab’s standard JavaScript API between a rich-media ad and its host app. The current version, MRAID 3.0, was finalised in June 2018.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://iabtechlab.com/standards/mobile-rich-media-ad-interface-definitions-mraid/"},{"claim":"Unity’s own playable-ad specification requires a single, inlined and minified index.html file, with no links to other files or folders, under 5 MB.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://docs.unity.com/en-us/grow/acquire/creatives/playable/specifications"},{"claim":"AppLovin requires each playable ad to be a single HTML file of 5 MB or smaller, with every resource embedded as base64 or base122; external resources are not allowed.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://support.applovin.com/en/growth/promoting-your-apps/welcome-to-applovin/creative-specs-and-guidelines"},{"claim":"For Google Ads App campaigns, an HTML5 or playable asset is uploaded as a single .ZIP of up to 5 MB containing no more than 512 files.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/9981650"},{"claim":"Meta accepts a playable either as one HTML5 file with every asset inlined as data URIs, or as a ZIP with index.html at its root and no more than 100 files, capped at 5 MB either way. It must work without mraid.js, make no external network calls or dynamic asset loads, and should call FbPlayableAd.onCTAClick() for the call to action.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://en-gb.facebook.com/business/help/412951382532338"},{"claim":"On 30 September 2020 the UK’s Advertising Standards Authority upheld complaints against two paid-for Facebook video ads by PLR Worldwide Sales Ltd t/a Playrix, for Homescapes and Gardenscapes, that showed pin-pulling puzzles. Despite on-screen text saying “Not all images represent actual gameplay”, it ruled them misleading: the games mostly consisted of match-three levels and a renovation storyline, and players had to get through a lot of different content to reach the featured puzzles. The ads must not appear again in that form.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://www.asa.org.uk/rulings/plr-worldwide-sales-ltd-g20-1061644-plr-worldwide-sales-ltd.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s web export produces separate .html, .js, .wasm and .pck files, plus a boot-splash .png, rather than one self-contained file; the docs note the WebAssembly engine module compresses to about a quarter of its original size with gzip.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/export/exporting_for_web.html"},{"claim":"An open Godot issue reported that in Godot 4.0 beta 4 the web export of a single empty 2D node came to over 40 MB, against over 20 MB in Godot 3.5, before any game asset was added.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/68647"},{"claim":"A June 2024 Deconstructor of Fun write-up on Voodoo’s own site, drawn from an interview with its VP Gaming, says Voodoo tests around 1,000 prototypes a year with roughly 100 in progress at once, and that only about 0.4%, around four games a year, reach full launch. In its hybrid-casual process a prototype aims for a sustainable return on ad spend of at least 150%, while retention and CPI are tuned, before soft launch.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://voodoo.io/news/voodoo-s-secret-sauce-from-0-to-250m-hybridcasual-revenue-in-3-years"},{"claim":"In a 15 February 2023 post, Supercell CEO Ilkka Paananen wrote that the company had launched 5 hit games but killed more than 30.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://supercell.com/en/news/next-chapter/"},{"claim":"Supercell put Rush Wars into beta on 26 August 2019, testing it in Finland, Australia, Canada and New Zealand.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://supercell.com/en/news/welcome-rush-wars/7177/"},{"claim":"On 5 November 2019 Supercell said it would not continue the Rush Wars beta: updates made after players called the game repetitive had not made it fun enough, and the team felt that even with more work it would not reach Supercell’s bar of a game played for years and remembered forever. The beta ran until 30 November 2019.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://supercell.com/en/news/rush-wars-closing/7225/"},{"claim":"GameAnalytics’ 2026 benchmark report, from a sample of over 16,000 mobile games (minimum 1,000 monthly active users) across 9 regions through 2025, put median Day 1 retention at around 22% (the top 1% of games reached 64 to 68%), median Day 7 at just under 4%, and median Day 30 at 0.7 to 0.8%. The report states genre-level breakdowns are not available in this edition, so treat any genre-specific D1/D7/D30 numbers quoted elsewhere as unverified for the current year.","asOf":"2026-09-25","src":"https://www.gameanalytics.com/reports/2026-mobile-pc-gaming-benchmarks"}]],["Ethics and responsibility","#/map/t/ethics-and-responsibility",[{"claim":"Apple’s App Store Review Guidelines (3.1.1) require apps that sell loot boxes or other randomised virtual items to disclose the odds of receiving each type of item before purchase, a rule in place since December 2017.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/"},{"claim":"Google Play has required games that sell randomised virtual items to disclose the odds before purchase since May 2019.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/games-on-the-google-play-store-now-required-to-disclose-loot-box-odds"},{"claim":"Since April 2020 the ESRB adds the notice “In-Game Purchases (Includes Random Items)” to games that sell randomised items for real money.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://www.esrb.org/blog/in-game-purchases-includes-random-items/"},{"claim":"In January 2025 Genshin Impact’s developer, Cognosphere, agreed to pay $20 million to settle FTC charges; the order bans selling loot boxes to players under 16 in the US without a parent’s consent.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/01/genshin-impact-game-developer-will-be-banned-selling-lootboxes-teens-under-16-without-parental"},{"claim":"The FTC’s amended COPPA Rule, published on 22 April 2025, requires full compliance from 22 April 2026: separate parental consent before a child’s data is disclosed to third parties such as advertisers, and biometric identifiers now count as personal information.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/04/22/2025-05904/childrens-online-privacy-protection-rule"}]],["Monetisation design","#/map/t/monetisation-design",[{"claim":"Apple’s App Store Review Guidelines (section 3.1.1) require apps that offer loot boxes or other mechanisms giving randomised virtual items for purchase to disclose the odds of receiving each type of item before the purchase. The requirement was added in December 2017.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/#in-app-purchase"},{"claim":"Google Play’s monetisation policy has required apps with randomised items from a purchase to clearly disclose the odds in advance of purchase since May 2019.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/google-play-developers-must-now-disclose-loot-box-odds"},{"claim":"In April 2018 the Belgian Gaming Commission concluded that loot boxes in FIFA 18, Counter-Strike: Global Offensive and Overwatch broke Belgian gambling law, and said publishers could face fines and prison (secondary source; the commission’s own report was not opened). Enforcement has since been patchy: a 2022 academic study reported that most of the 100 highest-grossing Belgian iPhone games still had loot boxes.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.pcgamesn.com/belgium-loot-box-laws-study"},{"claim":"On 9 March 2022 the Dutch Council of State (Administrative Jurisdiction Division) overturned the Netherlands Gambling Authority’s penalty payments against Electronic Arts over FIFA Ultimate Team player packs. It held that the packs were not a game of chance separate from the game mode they belong to, so they were not gambling on their own. Loot boxes are not automatically gambling under Dutch law. Source checked is a law firm summary; the Council of State page returned 403.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://akd.eu/insights/loot-boxes-are-legal-in-the-netherlands"},{"claim":"In April 2020 the ESRB began using an \"In-Game Purchases (Includes Random Items)\" interactive element, and PEGI began using an \"Includes Paid Random Items\" notice, for games that sell random items for real money.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://reedsmith.com/en/perspectives/2020/04/esrb-and-pegi-introduce-loot-box-warnings"},{"claim":"China’s Ministry of Culture rules, in force from 1 May 2017, required online game publishers to publish the odds of random-draw items. A draft of new NPPA online game measures issued on 22 December 2023 would have banned minors from loot boxes and capped spending, and was removed from the regulator’s website on 23 January 2024. Nothing final was enacted from it as far as the sources checked show. The 2017 rule rests on a secondary report; the PCGamesN page returned 403 on 2026-09-30.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.pcgamesn.com/loot-box-odds-china"}]],["Live design: seasons, cadence and reading the data","#/map/t/live-design-seasons-and-data",[{"claim":"Evan Miller's article How Not to Run an A/B Test shows that checking results repeatedly and stopping at the first significant reading raises false positives sharply: in his logo example (a 50 percent base conversion rate, a test run after every observation and stopped at 5 percent significance or 150 observations) the real false-positive rate was 26.1 percent, which he calls close to a worst case. His advice is to fix the sample size in advance and not stop early, or to use sequential or Bayesian designs built for repeated looks.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.evanmiller.org/how-not-to-run-an-ab-test.html"},{"claim":"Johari, Pekelis and Walsh (Optimizely; arXiv 1512.04922, submitted December 2015, revised July 2019) show an A/A test whose chance-to-beat-baseline crosses the 95 percent line under continuous monitoring, and state that with 10,000 samples the Type I error can easily increase fivefold. They propose always-valid p-values and the mixture sequential probability ratio test, which trade some run-time for valid error control. Two authors were employed by Optimizely, a testing vendor. Web click-through tests, not games.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.04922"},{"claim":"Deng, Xu, Kohavi and Walker (Microsoft, WSDM 2013) introduce CUPED and report a variance reduction of about 50 percent on Bing experiments, equal to doubling traffic or halving the run time. The gain peaked with about two weeks of experiment data, was best with the same metric from before the test, and requires pre-experiment data for a user. One search engine, not games.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.exp-platform.com/Documents/2013-02-CUPED-ImprovingSensitivityOfControlledExperiments.pdf"},{"claim":"Fabijan and colleagues (KDD 2019, authors from Microsoft, Booking.com and others; four companies, over 25 products) report that about 6 percent of Microsoft experiments show a sample ratio mismatch, cite about 10 percent of triggered analyses at LinkedIn, and give a taxonomy of causes with a chi-square test as the check. Web and app products, not games.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.lukasvermeer.nl/publications/papers/2019/07/25/diagnosing-sample-ratio-mismatch-in-online-controlled-experiments.html"},{"claim":"Julian Runge's Experimentation in Gaming: an Adoption Guide (arXiv 2504.13840, January 2025 draft) says new game content can cause novelty effects that excite players only because it is new and mislead about long-term retention, and advises a small long-term holdout and a look at effects by player tenure (novelty is lower or absent for new players). It is an advisory guide with no measured decay figures.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13840"}]],["Pitching a design to stakeholders","#/map/t/pitching-and-stakeholders",[{"claim":"Elsbach and Kramer (Academy of Management Journal 46(3), 2003, pp. 283-301) interviewed 36 film and television screenwriters, agents and producers (an initial 22 interviews, then 14 more) and observed 28 pitches, 16 of them enacted for the researchers. They report a dual-process model: person categorization, in which executives use behavioural and physical cues to match pitchers to seven creative and uncreative prototypes, and relationship categorization, in which relational cues and the executive's own reactions match pitchers to two relational prototypes. The authors note the industry is a small world with shared prototypes, and they study how judgments are formed, not whether they predict good projects. Not tested for games.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://leeds-faculty.colorado.edu/dahe7472/Elsbach%202003.pdf"}]],["AI for prototyping and implementation","#/map/t/ai-for-implementation",[{"claim":"Peng et al. (GitHub, February 2023): in a controlled experiment on one task, writing an HTTP server in JavaScript, the Copilot group finished 55.8% faster. It is a bounded greenfield task, not work inside a mature codebase.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06590"},{"claim":"METR (July 2025, revised February 2026): 16 experienced open-source developers on 246 tasks in mature repositories took 19% longer with AI allowed (interval +2% to +39%). Its 24 February 2026 update (57 developers, 800+ tasks) estimated -18% time for returning developers (interval -38% to +9%) and -4% for new ones (-15% to +9%), and METR called it only very weak evidence because of selection effects.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/"}]],["Generative assets: pipeline, provenance and rights","#/map/t/ai-generative-assets",[{"claim":"Steam’s content survey asks developers to disclose AI-generated content that ships with the game and reaches players (art, sound, narrative, localisation and any other content players see or hear), split into pre-generated content, made with AI tools during development, and live-generated content, made while the game runs. Efficiency gains from AI development tools are not covered. Valve rewrote the survey in January 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/contentsurvey"},{"claim":"The US Copyright Office’s January 2025 report: prompts alone do not make you the author of a model’s output; human selection, arrangement, modification or perceptible human-made material can be protected, case by case.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://copyright.gov/ai/Copyright-and-Artificial-Intelligence-Part-2-Copyrightability-Report.pdf"},{"claim":"SAG-AFTRA’s Interactive Media Agreement, ratified in July 2025, requires specific written consent and disclosure before a performer’s digital replica is created or used, and lets performers suspend consent for new AI material during a strike.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://www.sagaftra.org/sag-aftra-members-approve-2025-video-game-agreement"}]],["Disclosure, IP and platform rules for AI content","#/map/t/ai-disclosure-policy",[{"claim":"Steam’s content survey asks developers to disclose AI-generated content that ships with the game and reaches players (art, sound, narrative, localisation and any other content players see or hear), split into pre-generated content, made with AI tools during development, and live-generated content, made while the game runs. Efficiency gains from AI development tools are not covered. Valve rewrote the survey in January 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/contentsurvey"},{"claim":"The EU AI Act’s Article 50 transparency duties have applied since 2 August 2026: people must be told when they are interacting with an AI system unless it is obvious, and generative systems must mark synthetic content in a machine-readable way (systems already on the market have until 2 December 2026 for the marking).","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://www.goodwinlaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/08/alerts-technology-dpc-eu-ai-act-transparency-obligations-now-in-force"},{"claim":"The US Copyright Office’s January 2025 report: prompts alone do not make you the author of a model’s output; human selection, arrangement, modification or perceptible human-made material can be protected, case by case.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://copyright.gov/ai/Copyright-and-Artificial-Intelligence-Part-2-Copyrightability-Report.pdf"},{"claim":"SAG-AFTRA’s Interactive Media Agreement, ratified in July 2025, requires specific written consent and disclosure before a performer’s digital replica is created or used.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://www.sagaftra.org/sag-aftra-members-approve-2025-video-game-agreement"}]],["Generative characters: language models inside the game","#/map/t/generative-characters",[{"claim":"Steam’s content survey asks developers to disclose live-generated AI content, made while the game runs, separately from pre-generated content, and to describe the guardrails that keep it from generating illegal content. Efficiency gains from AI development tools are not covered. Valve rewrote the survey in January 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/contentsurvey"},{"claim":"The EU AI Act’s Article 50 duties have applied since 2 August 2026: a system that interacts with people must tell them they are dealing with an AI unless that is obvious from the context, and generative systems must mark synthetic output in a machine-readable way; systems already on the market have until 2 December 2026 for the marking.","asOf":"2026-09-23","src":"https://www.goodwinlaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/08/alerts-technology-dpc-eu-ai-act-transparency-obligations-now-in-force"}]],["Design critique and feedback","#/map/t/design-critique-and-feedback",[{"claim":"In Ed Catmull's Harvard Business Review article How Pixar Fosters Collective Creativity (2008), a brain trust of directors offers advice on works in progress; the production's leaders decide what to use, there are no mandatory notes, and the brain trust has no authority.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://hbr.org/2008/09/how-pixar-fosters-collective-creativity"},{"claim":"Kluger and DeNisi (Psychological Bulletin 119, 254-284, 1996) meta-analysed 607 effect sizes from 131 papers (23,663 observations). Feedback interventions raised performance on average (weighted d = .41) but over 38 percent of effects were negative, a spread not explained by sampling error. They propose that effects fall as feedback moves attention from the task to the self. Moderators: discouraging feedback, praise and threats to self-esteem weakened effects; feedback giving the correct solution or a rate of change strengthened them. Mostly lab and workplace tasks, not design work, and some moderators were significant only after exclusions. The study does not test critique of games, and the Braintrust practice has no controlled test.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://scholars.huji.ac.il/node/1802"}]],["Real-time connections at scale: gateways, pub/sub and fan-out","#/map/t/realtime-connection-tier-at-scale",[{"claim":"Discord’s 2017 post reports 5,000,000 concurrent users, a send/2 cost of 30 to 70 microseconds that makes a 30,000-user guild fan-out take 0.9 to 2.1 seconds, and a reconnect-burst ring lookup cost cut from 17.5 s to 750 ms by FastGlobal.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://discord.com/blog/how-discord-scaled-elixir-to-5-000-000-concurrent-users"},{"claim":"Discord’s large-guild post reports a server with over 1 million concurrent online users, about 90 per cent passive sessions, and relays handling up to about 15,000 sessions each.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://discord.com/blog/maxjourney-pushing-discords-limits-with-a-million-plus-online-users-in-a-single-server"},{"claim":"WhatsApp reported 2,277,845 open TCP connections on one 24-core, about 103 GB server in 2012 (FreeBSD 8.2, Erlang R14B03).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://blog.whatsapp.com/196/1-million-is-so-2011?lang=en"},{"claim":"Slack’s channel servers are mapped by consistent hashing, with a replacement ready in under 20 seconds and about 16 million channels per host at peak (a Slack channel here is an abstract id used for users, teams and files as well as chat rooms).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://slack.engineering/real-time-messaging/"},{"claim":"Redis Pub/Sub is at-most-once; sharded Pub/Sub (SSUBSCRIBE, SPUBLISH) exists from Redis 7.0 and keeps messages inside the shard that owns the slot.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/pubsub/"},{"claim":"A newly spawned Erlang process uses 327 words of memory, 233 of them initial heap including the stack.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.erlang.org/doc/system/eff_guide_processes.html"},{"claim":"Core NATS delivers a message at most once to interested connected subscribers; JetStream is the persistence layer. A server closes a connection that cannot take writes within its write deadline; the Go client’s default pending limit is 500,000 messages and 64 MB per subscription.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.nats.io/running-a-nats-service/nats_admin/slow_consumers; at-most-once statement: https://docs.nats.io/nats-concepts/core-nats"},{"claim":"FCM normal-priority messages may be delayed while a device is in Doze; high priority can wake it. The default time-to-live is four weeks (maximum 2,419,200 seconds).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/android/message-priority; TTL: https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/customize-messages/setting-message-lifespan"},{"claim":"APNs: payload limit 4 KB, apns-collapse-id up to 64 bytes, apns-priority 10 or 5 or 1.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/sending-notification-requests-to-apns"},{"claim":"WebSocket close code 1001 (going away) is defined in RFC 6455; 1013 (try again later) is in the IANA WebSocket close code registry.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.iana.org/assignments/websocket/websocket.xhtml"},{"claim":"The epoll man page puts the cost of each registered descriptor at about 160 bytes on a 64-bit kernel.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/epoll.7.html"},{"claim":"MQTT 5.0 defines QoS 0 (at most once), 1 (at least once) and 2 (exactly once), retained messages, session expiry and shared subscriptions.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.oasis-open.org/mqtt/mqtt/v5.0/mqtt-v5.0.html"}]],["Backend latency: find it, then cut it","#/map/t/backend-latency-and-query-optimisation",[{"claim":"Dean and Barroso (CACM, February 2013): for a server with a 1 second 99th percentile, a request that waits on 100 such servers is slower than a second 63 per cent of the time. A hedged request sent after a 10 ms delay cut the 99.9th percentile for reading 1,000 BigTable keys across 100 servers from 1,800 ms to 74 ms with 2 per cent more requests; waiting for the 95th percentile before hedging limits extra load to about 5 per cent.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.barroso.org/publications/TheTailAtScale.pdf"},{"claim":"Google's SRE book (Handling Overload) describes per-request retry limits of up to three attempts, a per-client retry ratio limit of 10 per cent, and adaptive client throttling that rejects locally once requests exceed K times accepts, with K = 2.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/"},{"claim":"Google's SRE book (Addressing Cascading Failures) advises keeping queues short relative to the thread pool (for example 50 per cent or less), setting deadlines high in the stack and propagating them with the elapsed time subtracted, and using randomised exponential backoff with a server-wide retry budget (a cap on retries per process).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://sre.google/sre-book/addressing-cascading-failures/"},{"claim":"PostgreSQL documentation: EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes the statement; the rows skipped by OFFSET still have to be computed inside the server; an index-only scan is a win only if a significant fraction of heap pages have their all-visible bit set; INCLUDE adds payload columns to an index.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html; https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-limit.html; https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-index-only-scans.html; https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createindex.html"},{"claim":"PostgreSQL defaults: shared_buffers 128 MB, work_mem 4 MB per sort or hash operation (so total memory can be many times work_mem). pg_stat_statements needs shared_preload_libraries and a restart, and tracks calls, total_exec_time and mean_exec_time per normalised statement.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-resource.html; pg_stat_statements: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgstatstatements.html"},{"claim":"Go's database/sql: the default maximum open connections is unlimited, the default maximum idle connections is 2 and may change; DB.Stats reports WaitCount and WaitDuration.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://pkg.go.dev/database/sql#DB.SetMaxOpenConns"},{"claim":"Go GC guide: GOGC defaults to 100, doubling it roughly halves GC CPU cost and doubles heap overhead; GOMEMLIMIT (Go 1.19 and later) is a soft memory limit.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://tip.golang.org/doc/gc-guide"},{"claim":"Godot's HTTPRequest.timeout defaults to 0.0 seconds, which means the request never times out; Unity's UnityWebRequest.timeout is an integer in seconds and the default 0 means no timeout.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_httprequest.html; Unity: https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Networking.UnityWebRequest-timeout.html"},{"claim":"PgBouncer transaction pooling assigns a server connection only for the duration of a transaction and breaks session-based features, including session-level advisory locks, LISTEN and SQL-level PREPARE. Protocol-level prepared statements are supported in transaction mode through max_prepared_statements (default 200 in the current docs).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.pgbouncer.org/features.html; https://www.pgbouncer.org/config.html"}]],["Authority models","#/map/t/server-authority",[{"claim":"Server-side lag compensation reconstructs the world at the time the shooter fired, using timestamped inputs, and evaluates the shot against it. Gambetta notes the cost: a target can take damage a fraction of a second after they thought they were safe behind cover.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.gabrielgambetta.com/lag-compensation.html"}]],["Rollback netcode and lockstep","#/map/t/server-rollback-netcode",[{"claim":"The GGPO README describes rollback as input prediction plus speculative execution: player inputs go to the game immediately.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://github.com/pond3r/ggpo"},{"claim":"The GGPO developer guide requires the game to implement save_game_state, load_game_state and advance_frame callbacks.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://github.com/pond3r/ggpo/blob/master/doc/DeveloperGuide.md"},{"claim":"Fiedler’s deterministic lockstep article: send only inputs, not state; the simulation must match to the bit across machines; a playout delay buffer absorbs jitter; and sending recent inputs redundantly in each UDP packet beats retransmission.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://gafferongames.com/post/deterministic_lockstep/"}]],["Choosing a multiplayer stack: hosting, services and netcode","#/map/t/server-stack-choices",[{"claim":"Unity’s Multiplay Game Server Hosting was deprecated on 1 April 2026, after a 31 March cut-off. From then customers could not scale new game servers or make new allocations, and customers who asked to move to Multiplay by Rocket Science could keep using it until their migration finished.","asOf":"2026-10-01","src":"https://status.unity.com/info_notices/362941"},{"claim":"Amazon GameLift Servers is AWS’s managed service for deploying, operating and scaling dedicated game servers for session-based multiplayer games, and includes FlexMatch matchmaking. It also offers Anywhere fleets, which run on your own hardware or another cloud.","asOf":"2026-10-01","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/gameliftservers/latest/developerguide/gamelift-intro.html"},{"claim":"Agones is an open-source platform that scales and orchestrates dedicated multiplayer game servers on anything that runs Kubernetes.","asOf":"2026-10-01","src":"https://agones.dev"},{"claim":"Nakama is an open-source game backend. Its server framework lets you write runtime code in Go, JavaScript or Lua.","asOf":"2026-10-01","src":"https://heroiclabs.com/docs/nakama/server-framework/introduction/"},{"claim":"Mirror, an open-source networking library for Unity, is MIT licensed.","asOf":"2026-10-01","src":"https://github.com/MirrorNetworking/Mirror"}]],["Bandwidth and interest management","#/map/t/server-bandwidth-and-interest-management",[{"claim":"Epic’s Replication Graph documentation says a Battle Royale match starts with 100 connected players and about 50,000 replicated actors, and that the standard strategy, in which each replicated actor decides for each connected client whether to send an update, bottlenecks the server’s CPU.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/replication-graph-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Unreal’s Iris is an opt-in replication system that runs alongside the legacy one. Epic’s overview page labels it experimental and enabled in four steps (the Iris plugin in the .uproject, SetupIrisSupport(Target) in the module’s .Build.cs, bUseIris = true in the .Target.cs, and entries in DefaultEngine.ini), with a runtime switch -UseIrisReplication. The legacy system stays the default.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/introduction-to-iris-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Unreal’s networking overview describes dormancy as controlling whether an actor is added to a connection’s list of actors considered for replication, and calls it one of the most significant network optimisations.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/networking-overview-for-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Godot’s MultiplayerSynchronizer has replication_interval and delta_interval (both default 0.0, meaning every network process frame), public_visibility (default true), add_visibility_filter() and set_visibility_for(), and a visibility_update_mode that defaults to updating every process frame.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_multiplayersynchronizer.html"},{"claim":"In Netcode for GameObjects, a NetworkObject hidden from a client is despawned there and generates no traffic for it. NetworkObject.CheckObjectVisibility is called when a client connects or just before spawn, and with no callback the object is visible to all clients.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://mp-docs.dl.it.unity3d.com/netcode/2.3.2/basics/object-visibility/"},{"claim":"Valve’s Source networking page gives cl_updaterate a default of 20, says the server never sends more updates than simulated ticks or the client’s requested rate, and that sv_minrate and sv_maxrate bound the client’s requested rate in bytes per second.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.valvesoftware.com/wiki/Source_Multiplayer_Networking"},{"claim":"Glenn Fiedler’s snapshot compression article starts from 17.38 Mbit/s for 901 cubes at 60 Hz and targets 256 kbit/s, using smallest-three orientation at 29 bits, positions at 50 bits, and delta encoding against an acknowledged baseline.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://gafferongames.com/post/snapshot_compression/"}]],["Transports: UDP, reliability, QUIC, WebRTC and relays","#/map/t/server-transport-and-relays",[{"claim":"RFC 9000 requires a client’s Initial packet to be sent in a UDP datagram of at least 1,200 bytes, assumes endpoints can send datagrams of 1,200 bytes unless they know more, limits an unvalidated server to three times the bytes it has received, and supports connection migration with connection ids and PATH_CHALLENGE path validation.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html"},{"claim":"RFC 9221 defines unreliable DATAGRAM frames for QUIC. They are not retransmitted, are subject to the connection’s congestion control, and the max_datagram_frame_size parameter defaults to 0, meaning unsupported until negotiated.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9221.html"},{"claim":"RFC 8656 (TURN) sets the default lifetime of an allocation at 10 minutes, permissions expire after 5 minutes unless refreshed, and a client can talk to the server over UDP, TCP, TLS or DTLS.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8656.html"},{"claim":"RFC 4787 requires a NAT’s UDP mapping to last at least two minutes without traffic and recommends five. Outbound traffic must refresh it.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4787.html"},{"claim":"RFC 8445 (ICE) defines host, peer-reflexive, server-reflexive and relayed candidates, with recommended type preferences of 126, 110, 100 and 0 in that order, and tests candidate pairs with STUN binding checks.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8445.html"},{"claim":"RFC 8200 requires every IPv6 link to have an MTU of at least 1,280 bytes, and IPv6 routers do not fragment packets.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8200.html"},{"claim":"WebRTC data channels run over SCTP over DTLS over UDP, can be ordered or unordered and partially reliable, and all channels of one connection share one SCTP association and congestion window (RFC 8831).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8831.html"},{"claim":"MDN lists WebTransport as Baseline 2026, newly available since March 2026 across current browsers, secure contexts only, with reliable streams and unreliable datagrams over HTTP/3. Check the compatibility table for your target browsers before relying on it.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebTransport_API"},{"claim":"Unity’s documentation says a Relay session supports up to 150 players joining the host, that the standalone Relay package is deprecated in Unity 6 in favour of the Multiplayer Services package, and that connection types are udp, dtls and wss. WebGL has no UDP, so DTLS relay is unavailable there.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity.com/en-us/mps-sdk/relay-limitations"},{"claim":"Unity Transport’s reliable pipeline stage defaults to a window of 32 packets in flight (maximum 64) and its fragmentation stage assumes an MTU of roughly 1,400 bytes.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.transport@2.4/manual/pipelines-usage.html"},{"claim":"Amazon GameLift’s player gateway is an opt-in relay that hides game server IP addresses, validates a token on all traffic and rate-limits each player. Enhanced DDoS Protection is on by default for fleets using Server SDK 5 and does not hide the server IP.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/gameliftservers/latest/developerguide/ddos-protection-intro.html"},{"claim":"Valve documents Steam Datagram Relay as preventing IP addresses from being revealed and protecting dedicated servers from denial-of-service attack, and says peer-to-peer connections are relayed over Valve’s backbone when appropriate. The GameNetworkingSockets README says SDR is not offered on other platforms to all partners.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/multiplayer/networking"}]],["Partitioning a world: shards, zones, instances and seamless servers","#/map/t/server-world-partitioning",[{"claim":"CCP’s 2010 dev blog on Character Nodes, which explains part of EVE’s architecture, says solar systems are the unit of load balancing, that each is statically assigned to a node running on one CPU core, and that a heavily loaded system cannot be split across cores. It gave 204 “sol” nodes in the cluster and said Jita had been held at about 1,400 pilots until the Character Nodes change, deployed through August 2010.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.eveonline.com/news/view/fixing-lag-character-nodes"},{"claim":"CCP’s April 2011 dev blog that introduced Time Dilation described it as slowing the game clock according to the queue of work waiting on a node. It used illustrative figures of about 5 percent of real time during an extreme warp-in, about 30 percent at 1,600 pilots and about 60 percent at 1,200. It excluded events tied to real clock times such as reinforcement timers, called the plan tentative, and said a hard lower limit would be needed without saying where it would sit.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.eveonline.com/news/view/introducing-time-dilation-tidi"},{"claim":"In the live game, time dilation bottoms out at 10 percent of real time, so one game second takes ten real seconds. This figure comes from EVE University’s wiki, a player-run secondary source, and not from CCP’s 2011 post.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://wiki.eveuniversity.org/Time_Dilation"},{"claim":"Kristjan Valur Jonsson of CCP gave a GDC China 2010 talk titled “The Server Technology of EVE Online: How to Cope with 300,000 Players in One World”, whose abstract says the technology and the game were built around one unified world rather than many shards.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://gdcvault.com/play/1014168/contactUs"},{"claim":"Epic’s Unreal Engine tech blog on Fortnite Battle Royale says the dedicated server had to handle 100 simultaneous players, maintain 20 Hz and minimise bandwidth.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.unrealengine.com/tech-blog/unreal-engine-improvements-for-fortnite-battle-royale"},{"claim":"Amazon’s AWS blog on New World says each world simulates more than 7,000 AI entities and hundreds of thousands of objects for 2,500 players, over a grid in which each hub covers two non-contiguous grid sections, with state handed from hub to hub as players move and DynamoDB handling about 800,000 writes every 30 seconds.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/gametech/the-unique-architecture-behind-amazon-games-seamless-mmo-new-world/"},{"claim":"Blizzard engineer Brian Birmingham said in an August 2019 Reddit AMA that each WoW Classic layer is a copy of the entire world, and that Blizzard was committed to reducing to one layer per realm before the second content phase.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://us.forums.blizzard.com/en/wow/t/layering-in-classic-blizzard-responds-on-reddit/260714"},{"claim":"Improbable said in August 2019 that three SpatialOS-based games had closed in three consecutive months, naming Worlds Adrift, Mavericks: Proving Grounds and Lazarus, and gave different reasons for each. The statement did not blame the platform.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.pcgamesinsider.biz/news/69519/ambitious-visionary-products-always-contain-some-risk-says-improbable-after-third-spatialos-project-bites-the-dust/"},{"claim":"The Agones documentation describes it as an open source platform on Kubernetes for deploying, hosting, scaling and orchestrating dedicated game servers, with allocation of a game server to a session.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://agones.dev/site/docs/overview/"}]],["Load testing and capacity planning","#/map/t/server-load-testing-and-capacity",[{"claim":"Google’s SRE book describes client-side adaptive throttling with a multiplier K of 2 over a two-minute window of requests and accepts, a per-request retry limit of about three attempts, a per-client retry ratio below about 10 per cent, and four criticality levels (CRITICAL_PLUS, CRITICAL, SHEDDABLE_PLUS, SHEDDABLE).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/"},{"claim":"Google’s SRE chapter on cascading failures recommends testing components until they break, testing recovery as well as breakage, using randomised exponential backoff, bounded queues, and deadline propagation, and describes the garbage-collection death spiral and cold caches as failure modes.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://sre.google/sre-book/addressing-cascading-failures/"},{"claim":"k6 defines six test types (smoke, average-load, stress, soak, spike, breakpoint) and its test-types page is the source for those six.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/testing-guides/test-types/"},{"claim":"k6 treats arrival-rate executors as the open model and constant-vus as the closed model; its documentation says a closed model couples the arrival rate to iteration time, which it names coordinated omission.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/using-k6/scenarios/concepts/open-vs-closed/"},{"claim":"Locust creates one user per simulated user, each in its own gevent green thread; its wait_time options include constant_throughput (per-user pacing) and poisson, and wait time can only constrain throughput, not launch more users.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.locust.io/en/stable/writing-a-locustfile.html"},{"claim":"Riot reported load testing VALORANT with a custom Go harness: about 10,000 simulated players per process on four CPUs, 200 containers for two million simulated players on a test shard, a three-hour run at full scale, a mock game server emulating over 500 games per core, and a doubling of player count between passing runs.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.riotgames.com/en/news/scalability-and-load-testing-valorant"},{"claim":"Heroic Labs’ Nakama was load tested to two million concurrent users with the Artillery tool on about 25,000 Fargate nodes, ramped over about 50 minutes, with scenarios running for four hours.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/gametech/how-code-wizards-load-tested-heroic-labs-nakama-to-two-million-concurrent-players-with-aws"},{"claim":"Google Cloud reported that Pokémon GO’s launch traffic quickly surged to 50 times the initial target, about 10 times the worst-case estimate; within 15 minutes of the launch in Australia and New Zealand, traffic had already passed expectations.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/bringing-pokemon-go-to-life-on-google-cloud"},{"claim":"AWS says that in a five-layer stack with three retries per layer, the database load can rise 243 times during failures, recommends retrying at a single point in the stack, and describes token-bucket retry limiting.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/"},{"claim":"AWS defines throughput as total requests sent and goodput as the part handled without errors and with low enough latency to be used, and tests far beyond the point where goodput breaks.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/using-load-shedding-to-avoid-overload/"},{"claim":"AWS’s jitter experiment compared full, equal and decorrelated jitter; full jitter used less work than equal jitter; all three jittered variants cut the work substantially, and full jitter more than halved the calls compared with exponential backoff without jitter in a 100-client contention simulation.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/exponential-backoff-and-jitter/"},{"claim":"Amazon GameLift’s target-based auto scaling keeps a PercentAvailableGameSessions buffer; its documentation says placing players in ready slots takes seconds while starting new instances and server processes can take minutes.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/gameliftservers/latest/developerguide/fleets-autoscaling-target.html"},{"claim":"Agones’ FleetAutoscaler policies include Buffer, Webhook, Counter and List (the reference lists seven types); the Buffer policy keeps bufferSize Ready game servers between minReplicas and maxReplicas, and the default sync interval is 30 seconds.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://agones.dev/site/docs/reference/fleetautoscaler/"},{"claim":"The Linux kernel documents a default ip_local_port_range of 32768 to 60999, and a net.core.somaxconn default of 4096 since Linux 5.4.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/networking/ip-sysctl.html"},{"claim":"getrlimit(2) documents that a process’s RLIMIT_NOFILE ceiling is bounded by /proc/sys/fs/nr_open; running out of descriptors fails with EMFILE.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/getrlimit.2.html"},{"claim":"The Go GC guide says GOGC defaults to 100, GOMEMLIMIT (Go 1.19) caps total runtime memory, and a limit set too low makes the program thrash in constant collection.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://tip.golang.org/doc/gc-guide"},{"claim":"Godot’s --headless flag sets the headless display and dummy audio drivers and is described as useful for servers; Godot’s default physics rate is 60 ticks per second.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_projectsettings.html"},{"claim":"Unity’s -batchmode runs with no display or input and -nographics skips graphics device initialisation.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/PlayerCommandLineArguments.html"},{"claim":"Gatling offers open-model injection steps (constantUsersPerSec, rampUsersPerSec, incrementUsersPerSec) and closed-model steps (constantConcurrentUsers, rampConcurrentUsers).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.gatling.io/concepts/injection/"}]],["Multiplayer frameworks compared: how each one works","#/map/t/server-framework-landscape",[{"claim":"Netcode for Entities (1.8 documentation; the package registry on 2026-10-03 lists 1.14.3 as the newest for Unity 2022.3 and 7.1.0 for Unity 6000.7) is described as a server-authoritative framework with client prediction. It sits on Unity’s Entity Component System, supports Unity 2022.3 LTS and Unity 6 LTS, and its prediction default is a 60 Hz simulation tick rate and a command slack of 2 ticks. The prediction page calls prediction very CPU intensive because clients re-simulate multiple ticks per snapshot.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.netcode@1.8/manual/intro-to-prediction.html"},{"claim":"Netcode for GameObjects (2.3.2 documentation) supports two topologies, client-server and distributed authority. Distributed authority spreads ownership of distributable NetworkObjects across clients, uses a session owner that is re-selected if that client leaves, depends on the Multiplayer Services package, and Unity says it is not suitable for high-performance competitive games.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://mp-docs.dl.it.unity3d.com/netcode/2.3.2/terms-concepts/distributed-authority/"},{"claim":"Unity package registry, read 2026-10-03: every Netcode for GameObjects 2.x release requires Unity 6 (the 2.0.0 manifest requires 6000.0), so 1.x is the line for 2021.3 and 2022.3. The newest releases were 2.13.3 (6000.0) and 3.1.0 (6000.7). The NGO 2.4 manual says it supports Unity 6.0 and later and describes no client prediction or rollback.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://packages.unity.com/com.unity.netcode.gameobjects"},{"claim":"Photon’s public pricing page lists a free 20 CCU development plan, a free 100 CCU launch plan for one app (about 40,000 MAU, 0.3 TB traffic a month), paid monthly plans of 500 CCU at 125 dollars, 1,000 CCU at 250 dollars and 2,000 CCU at 500 dollars, and a premium cloud at 0.50 dollars per CCU with a 1,000 dollar monthly minimum, adapting to usage up to 50,000 CCU. Enterprise terms sit above that.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.photonengine.com/fusion/pricing"},{"claim":"Photon announced on 11 March 2024 that Fusion and Quantum have a free 100 CCU plan, up from 20, for one application per studio, and that it cannot be combined with paid CCU plans.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://blog.photonengine.com/?p=5466"},{"claim":"Photon’s Fusion 2 documentation lists three topologies, dedicated server, client-host and shared authority, with client-side prediction and lag compensation in the server-authoritative modes and an area-of-interest system. In shared authority each client has state authority over the objects it spawns, and the Photon room controls changes of state authority. Photon Quantum 3 is documented as a deterministic ECS for up to 128 players, using predict/rollback on input only.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://doc-us-test.photonengine.com/quantum/current/quantum-intro"},{"claim":"Mirror is MIT licensed, supports Unity 2019 to 2022 LTS and 6000.1, and lists server and client authority, interest management, snapshot interpolation and multiple transports (UDP, TCP, WebSockets, Steam, relay). Its README cites a 2019 test with 480 CCU as worst case and a 2022 test at 400 to 800 CCU.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://github.com/MirrorNetworking/Mirror"},{"claim":"Fish-Networking is free, server-authoritative by design, and its documentation says it has no CCU caps or paywalls and allows any server host. Its documentation describes a Transport system for any topology and a prediction feature, defined as server-authoritative actions while clients move in real time without delay.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://fish-networking.gitbook.io/docs"},{"claim":"Unreal Replication Graph: standard replication makes each replicated actor decide whether to update each connected client, a CPU bottleneck at scale; the plugin uses persistent nodes to build replication lists, and Epic cites Fortnite Battle Royale with 100 players and about 50,000 replicated actors.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/replication-graph-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Godot Engine is released under the MIT licence.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://godotengine.org/license/"},{"claim":"Unreal Engine’s documentation describes a server-authoritative client-server model with three replication systems: the default generic system, Replication Graph and Iris. Iris is marked Experimental, is opt-in, and is described as built on experience from a game with up to 100 players per server.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/introduction-to-iris-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Godot’s high-level multiplayer provides ENet (default), WebRTC and WebSocket peers. The default authority is the server, RPC modes are authority and any_peer, and transfer modes are unreliable, unreliable_ordered and reliable. MultiplayerSynchronizer replication_interval and delta_interval default to 0.0, meaning every network frame.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/networking/high_level_multiplayer.html"},{"claim":"Nakama is Apache-2.0 licensed, names CockroachDB as its database in its README (other database support was not checked), and its runtime code can be written in Lua, TypeScript/JavaScript or Go. It offers relayed multiplayer, where the server does not inspect the data, and authoritative multiplayer. Heroic Cloud is the managed offering.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://heroiclabs.com/docs/nakama/concepts/multiplayer/"},{"claim":"Colyseus is an MIT-licensed Node.js framework with authoritative rooms. State is defined with schema decorators and sent as binary delta patches, by default every 50 ms (20 per second). It has official SDKs for web, Unity, Godot, Defold, GameMaker, Construct and Haxe, and a managed Colyseus Cloud.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.colyseus.io/"},{"claim":"Steam’s networking sockets relay packets through Valve’s network by default so that players and servers are protected, support messages larger than one packet and reliable delivery, and may use a direct connection when appropriate. The documentation page states no developer cost.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/multiplayer/networking"},{"claim":"Epic Online Services was announced in a 2021 post as free for any engine, store or platform, with services including matchmaking, lobbies, leaderboards, voice (relayed through Epic’s servers) and anti-cheat.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://onlineservices.epicgames.com/news/epic-online-services-launches-two-new-free-services"},{"claim":"Amazon GameLift Servers has managed EC2 fleets, managed container fleets and Anywhere fleets (your own computes registered with the service). All fleets support FlexMatch matchmaking through a placement queue. Its pricing page bills per instance hour (an example c6a.4xlarge at 0.741 dollars an hour), lists Spot savings of 50% to 85%, and says network bandwidth is free for instance generation 6 and later.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://aws.amazon.com/gamelift/servers/pricing/"},{"claim":"Agones is an Apache-2.0 open-source platform on Kubernetes that adds GameServer, Fleet, FleetAutoscaler and GameServerAllocation resources. It ships no matchmaker; its documentation has integration patterns showing how an external matchmaker allocates a server through GameServerAllocation.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://agones.dev/site/docs/overview/"},{"claim":"Edgegap’s pricing page lists on-demand edge hosting at 0.00115 dollars per vCPU per minute while active, egress at 0.10 dollars per GB, reserved bare metal from 0.0256 dollars per vCPU per hour, and relays from 8 dollars a month for 100 CCU to 450 dollars for 2,000 CCU. It says relays carry no compute and are not authoritative servers.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://edgegap.com/pricing"},{"claim":"Reported, not confirmed by a vendor page: Hathora was acquired by Fireworks AI (announced 4 March 2026), Hathora Cloud game hosting ended on 5 May 2026, and customers were pointed to Nitrado. The readable source is a competing host’s blog; the vendor domain redirects to Nitrado’s product. Recheck before citing it as a date.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://crux.supercraft.host/blog/hathora-shut-down-where-to-go-after-may-2026/"},{"claim":"Socket.IO starts on HTTP long-polling and upgrades to WebSocket (its how-it-works page also lists WebTransport as a built-in transport), with a default ping interval of 25 seconds and pong timeout of 20 seconds. Its default delivery is at-most-once, with no resend after a dropped connection; at-least-once needs the retries option or application-level event ids and offsets.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://socket.io/docs/v4/delivery-guarantees/"},{"claim":"Phoenix Channels use WebSocket or long polling, route by topic, spread across nodes through PubSub, and deliver at most once: a client that is offline misses the message.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://phoenix.hexdocs.pm/channels.html"},{"claim":"Ably’s pricing page lists a free tier of 6,000,000 messages a month, 200 concurrent connections and 200 concurrent channels, and pay-as-you-go prices of 2.50 dollars per million messages, 1.00 dollar per million connection minutes and 1.00 dollar per million channel minutes before volume discounts, with Standard at 29 dollars a month and Pro at 399 dollars a month.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://ably.com/docs/platform/pricing"},{"claim":"Firebase Realtime Database allows 200,000 simultaneous connections per database on standard plans (100 on the free Spark plan) and about 1,000 writes a second per database, above which writes may be rate limited. Firebase recommends sharding across databases beyond that.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://firebase.google.com/docs/database/usage/limits"}]],["Cross-discipline handoffs and dependencies","#/map/t/pm-cross-discipline",[{"claim":"Nagappan, Murphy and Basili (Microsoft Research MSR-TR-2008-11, January 2008; ICSE 2008) studied Windows Vista and report that organisational-complexity metrics were significantly better predictors of failure-proneness, in precision and recall, than churn, complexity, coverage, dependencies and pre-release bugs. One large software product and a correlational case study, so generalisation beyond it is limited. It supports the claim that who works on and hands over a component affects quality; it does not test any particular handoff artifact and is not about games.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/the-influence-of-organizational-structure-on-software-quality-an-empirical-case-study/"}]],["Feedback and performance management","#/map/t/lead-feedback-performance",[{"claim":"UK government guidance on dismissal describes fair reasons and procedures, which is an example of employment rules that differ by country.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.gov.uk/dismissal"},{"claim":"The Acas code of practice on disciplinary and grievance procedures expects written notice of the problem, a meeting, the right to be accompanied and an appeal; tribunals can adjust awards by up to 25 per cent for unreasonable failure to follow it.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.acas.org.uk/acas-code-of-practice-on-disciplinary-and-grievance-procedures/html"},{"claim":"Radical Candor is a feedback framework by Kim Scott built on two axes: care personally and challenge directly. Its site names the failure modes ruinous empathy, obnoxious aggression and manipulative insincerity.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.radicalcandor.com/our-approach/"}]],["Conflict, delegation and growing people","#/map/t/lead-conflict-growth",[{"claim":"Will Larson’s site lethain.com publishes essays on engineering management.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://lethain.com/"}]],["Growing juniors when AI writes the code","#/map/t/lead-junior-skill-formation-with-ai",[{"claim":"Anthropic, \"How AI assistance impacts the formation of coding skills\" (29 January 2026): randomised trial, 52 mostly junior engineers learning the Trio async library, then a quiz without help. AI group 50%, hand-coding group 67% (gap 17 points, Cohen’s d 0.738, p = 0.01); the AI group was about two minutes faster, not significant. Delegating code generation and repeated AI debugging scored below 40%; asking for explanations or conceptual questions scored 65% or more. Vendor-run, small, quiz taken straight after the task.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/AI-assistance-coding-skills"},{"claim":"Cui et al., three field experiments at Microsoft, Accenture and a Fortune 100 firm (4,867 developers, Copilot, 2 to 8 months): +26.08% completed tasks; less experienced developers adopted the tool more and gained more. Output measured, not learning, and not quality.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/draft_copilot_experiments.pdf"}]],["Choosing platforms","#/map/t/platform-choice",[{"claim":"Steam’s Deck/Machine compatibility review is optional and sorts a game into Verified, Playable, Unsupported or Unknown, checking input, performance, seamlessness and display; you request it from the app’s Technical Tools or it can be queued automatically.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/steamdeck/compat"},{"claim":"Godot has no official console export because console SDKs are NDA-bound and Godot is MIT-licensed; the Godot Foundation instead lists console middleware (W4 Games, RAWRLAB Games) and porting houses.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://godotengine.org/consoles/"},{"claim":"W4 Consoles supports Switch, PS5 and Xbox Series X|S, with Switch 2 in early beta; the Starter plan costs $800 per platform or $2,000 for all platforms a year, and is limited to companies with up to 30 staff and under $300,000 in revenue or funding.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://www.w4games.com/w4consoles"},{"claim":"From SDK v74, Meta names OpenXR a recommended path for Unity, Unreal and Godot on Quest.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/blog/openxr-standard-quest-horizonos-unity-unreal-godot-developer-success/"}]],["Getting access: programmes, NDAs and dev kits","#/map/t/platform-access",[{"claim":"Steam Direct’s fee is $100 per app, recoupable once the product reaches at least $1,000 Adjusted Gross Revenue.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/appfee"},{"claim":"Nintendo Developer Portal registration is free and open to individuals; access to Switch-specific information requires a separate application submitted after registering and accepting the NDA and Terms of Service.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.nintendo.com/the-process"},{"claim":"PlayStation Partners registration’s first question asks where the applicant’s company or institution is registered, indicating a registered entity is generally expected to apply.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://register.playstation.net/"},{"claim":"ID@Xbox requires being at least 18 years old, signing an NDA, and being in a country with which Microsoft can do business.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.microsoft.com/en-us/games/publish"},{"claim":"The full console GDK and NDA samples further require an organisation onboarded with a Partner Center account and a Microsoft Entra ID tenant, since personal Microsoft accounts are no longer supported.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/gdk-dev/development-downloads/access-resources"},{"claim":"Apple Developer Program membership costs $99 per year; an organisation account additionally requires a D-U-N-S Number, legal binding authority, a work email on the organisation’s domain, and a public website.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/programs/enroll/"}]],["Platform requirements: what every build must do","#/map/t/platform-requirements",[{"claim":"Xbox Requirement XR-055 requires games to launch with at least 10 and at most 100 achievements worth 1,000 gamerscore, with semi-annual additions up to 100 achievements / 1,000 G and a lifetime cap of 500 achievements / 5,000 G; demos cannot have achievements.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/store/policies/console/certification-requirements"},{"claim":"Xbox Requirement XR-130 requires supporting the whole console generation, Xbox Series X and Series S, with equal modes and no player split between them.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/store/policies/console/certification-requirements"},{"claim":"Google Play requires new apps and updates to target Android 16 (API level 36) from 2026-08-31, with an extension to 2026-11-01 available on request; existing apps must target at least API 35 to remain visible to new users on newer Android.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/11926878"},{"claim":"Since 2025-11-01, new Android apps and updates targeting Android 15+ with native code must support 16 KB memory pages, which affects native libraries built by Unity, Unreal and Godot.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://android-developers.googleblog.com/2025/05/prepare-play-apps-for-devices-with-16kb-page-size.html"},{"claim":"Since 2026-04-28, Apple requires apps to be built with Xcode 26 and the iOS 26 SDK; since 2026-09-09, iOS and iPadOS apps must target iOS 13 or later.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/news/upcoming-requirements/"}]],["Certification and store review","#/map/t/certification-and-review",[{"claim":"Steam’s store page review and build review each typically take 3 to 5 business days; Valve recommends submitting each at least 7 business days before launch.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/store/review_process"},{"claim":"Xbox certification SLAs are a digital base final in 4 business days (Fastlane 48 hours), a digital content update in 2 business days (Fastlane 24 hours), and a disc submission in 5 business days.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/game-publishing/concepts/certification/certification-guide"},{"claim":"Apple states that on average 90% of App Review submissions are reviewed in less than 24 hours, with expedited review available for critical fixes and event-tied releases.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/distribute/app-review/"},{"claim":"Google Play review times can run up to seven days or longer in exceptional cases for certain developer accounts.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859751"},{"claim":"Nintendo reviews every product before release and publishes no turnaround time.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.nintendo.com/the-process"}]],["Ratings, privacy forms and disclosures","#/map/t/ratings-and-disclosures",[{"claim":"IARC’s rating authorities are ACB (Australia), ClassInd (Brazil), Taiwan DGSC, ESRB (US/Canada), GAMR (Saudi Arabia), GRAC (Korea), IGRS (Indonesia), PEGI (UK/Europe) and USK (Germany). CERO (Japan) is not listed, so console releases in Japan need a CERO rating obtained directly.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://globalratings.com/participants/"},{"claim":"Apple runs its own age-rating questionnaire rather than IARC, with tiers 4+, 9+, 13+, 16+ and 18+ (the 13+/16+/18+ tiers and new questions on in-app controls, capabilities, medical/wellness and violent themes were added in this cycle); developers had to answer the new questions by 2026-01-31 to avoid interruption to updates.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/news/?id=ks775ehf"},{"claim":"Google Play requires a Data safety form for every app on closed, open or production tracks, including apps that collect no user data themselves, and the form must cover data collected by third-party SDKs; a privacy policy link is also required.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/10787469"},{"claim":"Microsoft Store policy 10.8.4 requires disclosing the odds of receiving each item from a paid random item, in the product, on the product page, or on a linked website (Store Policies version 7.20, dated 2026-09-14).","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/store-policies"},{"claim":"PEGI’s expanded age-rating criteria take effect June 2026: games with paid random items default to PEGI 16 (some to PEGI 18), and other new interactive-risk categories cover time-limited purchase offers, blockchain/NFT mechanics and unrestricted player communication.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://pegi.info/news/pegi-expands-age-rating-criteria-interactive-risk-categories"}]],["Store pages, pricing and getting paid","#/map/t/store-presence",[{"claim":"Steam’s revenue share is reported as 30% up to $10M in lifetime revenue, 25% from $10M to $50M, and 20% above $50M, counting packages, DLC, in-game sales and Marketplace fees together.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://gameinformer.com/2018/11/30/valve-adjusting-revenue-share-for-steams-most-popular-games"},{"claim":"Apple’s App Store commission is 30% standard, or 15% on auto-renewable subscriptions after a subscriber’s first year of paid service. Members of Apple’s Small Business Program (no more than $1 million in proceeds in the prior calendar year, or new to the App Store; enrolment required) pay 15% on paid apps and in-app purchases.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/app-store/small-business-program/"},{"claim":"Microsoft Store charges a 12% fee on games sold through its own commerce platform on PC (15% for apps), with no registration fee under its current onboarding flow.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/get-started"},{"claim":"Developers keep 100% of the first $1M in net revenue per product per year on the Epic Games Store (the threshold resets each January 1), then splits 88/12 above that, for payments Epic processes (the opt-in Epic First Run program instead gives 100% for six months in exchange for exclusivity); there is a recoupable $100 submission fee per game.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://store.epicgames.com/en-US/news/epic-games-store-updates-revenue-share-keep-100-of-the-first-1m-per-product-per-year"},{"claim":"Google Play’s new fee structure lowers the rate on a developer’s first $1M of annual earnings to 10%, plus a billing fee where it applies, phasing in by region: US/UK/EEA from 2026-06-30, Australia and Japan from 2026-09-30, South Korea from 2026-12-31, and the rest of the world from 2027-09-30.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/16954621"}]],["Release day, patches and early access","#/map/t/release-and-updates",[{"claim":"Xbox certification SLAs are: digital base final 4 business days (Fastlane 48 hours), digital content update 2 business days (Fastlane 24 hours), and disc submission 5 business days.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/game-publishing/concepts/certification/certification-guide"},{"claim":"Apple’s phased release ramps an update over about 7 days in steps of roughly 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%, 50% and 100% of users who have automatic updates on, and can be paused for up to 30 days in total.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/update-your-app/release-a-version-update-in-phases"},{"claim":"On the Meta Horizon Store, a binary uploaded to the Production release channel updates immediately and without review, while metadata changes are reviewed and usually take 1 to 2 business days.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/resources/publish-after-sub/"},{"claim":"Steam Early Access requires the game to be playable at the time of listing, forbids specific promises about future updates, and requires the price not exceed what it costs on other stores, with release on Steam no later than anywhere else.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/store/earlyaccess"}]],["UGC platforms: Roblox and Fortnite","#/map/t/ugc-platforms",[{"claim":"Roblox accounts are split into Roblox Kids (5 to 8), Roblox Select (9 to 15) and Roblox (16 and over), and a game must pass an evaluation before under-16s can see it.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/publishing/kids-and-select"},{"claim":"Roblox’s Developer Exchange pays $0.0038 per Earned Robux for Robux earned from 2025-09-05 (older balances cash out at $0.0035), and needs age 13 or over and at least 30,000 Earned Robux.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/monetization/developer-exchange"},{"claim":"Fortnite’s Engagement Pool receives 40% of eligible net revenue from the Item Shop and related real-money purchases.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/fortnite/engagement-payout-in-fortnite-creative"},{"claim":"Fortnite creators receive 50% of the V-Bucks value of in-island sales, raised to 100% from 2026-01-09 through 2027-01-31.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/fortnite/in-island-transactions-overview-in-fortnite"}]],["Choosing an engine","#/map/t/platforms-choosing-an-engine",[{"claim":"Godot is MIT-licensed with no royalties; the Godot Foundation maintains no official console ports, and third parties such as W4 Games offer them.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://godotengine.org/consoles/"},{"claim":"Unity Personal is free up to $200,000 of revenue and funding, and Pro is required above that.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/products/pricing-updates"},{"claim":"Building for PlayStation, Xbox and Nintendo platforms needs an active Unity Pro subscription or a Preferred Platform licence key from the platform holder, plus developer approval from each platform holder.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/solutions/console"},{"claim":"Unity cancelled the Runtime Fee for games on 12 September 2024.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/blog/unity-is-canceling-the-runtime-fee"},{"claim":"Unreal charges a 5% royalty on lifetime gross revenue above $1 million attributable to the product; Epic Games Store sales are royalty-free.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.unrealengine.com/license"},{"claim":"GameMaker Professional is a $99.99 one-time commercial licence without console export; console export needs Enterprise.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/help/articles/november-2023-pricing-terms-change-faq"},{"claim":"The Defold License is derived from Apache 2.0: games made with Defold may be commercialised, the editor and engine may not.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/license/"},{"claim":"Nintendo Switch support is not in the standard Defold version; registered Nintendo developers sign up for it on the Tools and Middleware page.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/manuals/nintendo-switch/"},{"claim":"The cocos-engine repository was already MIT-licensed in the 3.8 line, and its v3.8.8 tag carries the licence.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/cocos/cocos-engine/blob/v3.8.8/LICENSE.md"},{"claim":"On 4 January 2026 SUD, which owns Cocos, announced COCOS 4 in a press release as fully open source under MIT, separating the engine from the editor; its GitHub releases are still 4.0.0 alphas.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/cocos-4-is-here-fully-open-source-302652264.html"},{"claim":"The WeChat mini game package cannot exceed 4 MB including code and assets, with subpackages supported.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.cocos.com/creator/manual/en/editor/publish/publish-wechatgame.html"},{"claim":"Most of Ren’Py is MIT-licensed, with portions derived from LGPL code.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/license.html"}]],["Tokens, embeddings and the context window","#/map/t/models-tokens-context",[{"claim":"Anthropic’s pricing page gives a rough estimate of one token per about 4 characters or 0.75 words of English, varying by language and content.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"},{"claim":"Claude Sonnet 5 and Sonnet 5.5 are each priced at $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens; Claude Haiku 4.5 at $1 and $5.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"},{"claim":"Claude 4.7 and later use a newer tokenizer that produces approximately 30% more tokens for the same text, depending on content.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"},{"claim":"Claude 4.6 and later models include a 1M-token context window at standard per-token pricing.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"},{"claim":"Anthropic prompt-cache reads cost 0.1x the base input price on most models (0.05x on Opus 5.5, 0.025x on Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1), 5-minute cache writes cost 1.25x and 1-hour writes 2x; the Batch API is 50% off input and output.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"}]],["Attention, the KV cache and long context","#/map/t/models-attention-long-context",[{"claim":"Liu et al., \"Lost in the Middle\" (2023, TACL), found performance is often highest when relevant information is at the beginning or end of the input and degrades significantly in the middle, even for explicitly long-context models.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172"},{"claim":"Chroma’s \"Context Rot\" report (published 14 July 2025, revised 16 July) evaluated 18 models, including GPT-4.1, Claude 4, Gemini 2.5 and Qwen3, and found performance declines as input length grows, with distractors compounding the loss.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot"},{"claim":"In the same report, models performed better on shuffled haystacks than on logically coherent ones, a finding the authors did not fully explain.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot"}]],["Sampling, temperature and determinism","#/map/t/models-sampling",[{"claim":"Thinking Machines Lab (Horace He, 10 September 2025) sampled 1,000 completions of one prompt from Qwen3-235B at temperature 0 and got 80 unique outputs; with batch-invariant kernels all 1,000 were identical.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/defeating-nondeterminism-in-llm-inference/"},{"claim":"The same post argues the primary cause of nondeterminism on inference endpoints is lack of batch invariance, because server load changes batch size, rather than floating-point concurrency alone.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/defeating-nondeterminism-in-llm-inference/"},{"claim":"Anthropic’s Messages API reference says that models released after Claude Opus 4.6 do not support setting temperature: 1.0 is accepted for backwards compatibility and any other value is rejected with a 400 error.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/messages"}]],["Hallucination and sycophancy","#/map/t/models-hallucination-sycophancy",[{"claim":"Kalai, Nachum, Vempala and Zhang (OpenAI and Georgia Tech), \"Why Language Models Hallucinate\" (arXiv, 4 September 2025), argue hallucinations originate in pretraining and persist because most evaluations reward guessing over acknowledging uncertainty.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04664"},{"claim":"Sharma et al. (Anthropic), \"Towards Understanding Sycophancy in Language Models\" (arXiv, October 2023), found five state-of-the-art assistants consistently sycophantic across four tasks, and that humans and preference models sometimes prefer convincing sycophantic answers over correct ones.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13548"},{"claim":"OpenAI’s GPT-5 System Card (13 August 2025), SimpleQA without web: gpt-5-thinking-mini scored 0.22 accuracy with a 0.26 hallucination rate, against 0.24 and 0.75 for o4-mini; the card attributes the difference to significantly better abstention behaviour.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://cdn.openai.com/gpt-5-system-card.pdf"}]],["How models are made","#/map/t/models-how-models-are-made",[{"claim":"InstructGPT: outputs of a 1.3B feedback-trained model were preferred by labellers over the 175B GPT-3 base model.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155"},{"claim":"LoRA reduced trainable parameters by 10,000 times and GPU memory by 3 times versus full fine-tuning of GPT-3 175B, with no added inference latency.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685"},{"claim":"QLoRA fine-tuned a 65B model on a single 48GB GPU using 4-bit NormalFloat weights while matching 16-bit fine-tuning performance on the paper’s benchmarks.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314"},{"claim":"DPO trains on preference pairs directly, removing the separate reward model and the RL sampling loop of RLHF.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18290"},{"claim":"Constitutional AI replaces human harmlessness labels with a list of principles and AI-generated preference comparisons (RLAIF).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08073"},{"claim":"GPTQ quantised 175B models to 3 or 4 bits per weight with negligible accuracy degradation on its benchmarks.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.17323"},{"claim":"An empirical study of LLaMA3 quantisation from 1 to 8 bits found non-negligible degradation, worst at ultra-low bit widths.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.14047"},{"claim":"A study of quantisation on long-context tasks found 8-bit kept accuracy within about 0.8%, while 4-bit methods lost up to 59% on some long-context tasks, with larger drops when the input was not English.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20276"},{"claim":"A study of quantised multilingual models found non-Latin-script languages hurt most and mathematical reasoning degrading fastest; human raters saw larger drops than automatic benchmarks.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.03211"}]],["Architecture choices that matter to users","#/map/t/models-architecture-choices",[{"claim":"Mixtral 8x7B has 8 experts per layer with 2 used per token: 47B total parameters, 13B active per token.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04088"},{"claim":"The Mixtral paper states that serving memory is proportional to the 47B sparse parameter count, and that routing and loading more than one expert per device add overhead, so the design suits batched workloads.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04088"},{"claim":"Claude bills an image at one visual token per 28 by 28 pixel patch: a 1920 by 1080 screenshot costs about 1,560 tokens on standard-resolution models and 2,691 on high-resolution ones.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/vision"}]],["Open weights, closed models and routing","#/map/t/models-open-and-closed",[{"claim":"Llama 3.1 Community License: licensees with over 700 million monthly active users on the release date must request a separate licence; products must display \"Built with Llama\"; derivative model names must begin with \"Llama\"; an Acceptable Use Policy is incorporated.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/meta-llama/llama-models/main/models/llama3_1/LICENSE"},{"claim":"The OSI Open Source AI Definition 1.0 requires data information, complete code and parameters, not weights alone.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://opensource.org/ai/open-source-ai-definition"},{"claim":"Apple’s 2025 on-device foundation model has about 3 billion parameters compressed to 2 bits per weight with quantisation-aware training, with LoRA adapters used to recover quality.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/apple-foundation-models-2025-updates"},{"claim":"FrugalGPT reported a cascade matching GPT-4 with up to 98% cost reduction on its benchmarks.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.05176"}]],["Prompt caching","#/map/t/models-prompt-caching",[{"claim":"Anthropic: cache lifetime is 5 minutes by default with an optional 1 hour; 5-minute writes cost 1.25x and 1-hour writes 2x the base input price; reads cost 0.1x on most models (0.05x on Claude Opus 5.5).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"},{"claim":"Anthropic: up to 4 cache breakpoints per request; the prefix order is tools, then system, then messages, and a change at one level invalidates it and all later levels.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"},{"claim":"Anthropic: minimum cacheable length depends on the model, for example 512 tokens on Claude Opus 5.5, 1,024 on Sonnet 5 and 4,096 on Haiku 4.5; shorter prompts are not cached and no error is returned.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"},{"claim":"Anthropic: automatic prefix checking looks back at most 20 blocks from a breakpoint.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"},{"claim":"OpenAI (GPT-5.6 and later): caching applies from 1,024 input tokens; cached reads cost 0.1x and writes 1.25x the uncached input rate; entries are kept 30 minutes after the last write or reuse.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching"},{"claim":"OpenAI: a hit requires the whole rendered prefix to match exactly; changing the model, tools, tool order, schemas, output format or reasoning effort resets it.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching"}]],["Context engineering","#/map/t/models-context-engineering",[{"claim":"Anthropic defines context engineering as the strategies for curating and maintaining the optimal set of tokens during LLM inference, an evolution of prompt engineering for multi-turn agents.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"},{"claim":"Claude Code starts each session with a fresh context window; CLAUDE.md files and auto memory (first 200 lines or 25KB) are loaded at the start; the docs target under 200 lines per CLAUDE.md and say instructions are context, not enforced configuration.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory"},{"claim":"Claude Code sends the full conversation with every request, auto-compacts history near the context limit, and notes that /compact is itself a large request while /clear costs nothing.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/costs"}]],["Agent harnesses: loop, tools, sub-agents, memory","#/map/t/models-agent-harnesses",[{"claim":"Anthropic’s \"Building effective agents\" (19 Dec 2024) distinguishes workflows (predefined code paths) from agents (models directing their own process and tool use), advises starting simple, and recommends stopping conditions such as a maximum number of iterations.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents"},{"claim":"Anthropic reported that for its SWE-bench agent it spent more time optimising tools than the overall prompt, and that requiring absolute file paths removed relative-path mistakes.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents"},{"claim":"Anthropic reported agents using about 4x the tokens of chat and its multi-agent research system about 15x (June 2025).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system"},{"claim":"In Claude Code each sub-agent runs in its own context window, returns only its summary to the parent, can have its tools restricted, and sends its own API requests that count toward usage.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents"},{"claim":"Claude Code documents agent teams using approximately 7x the tokens of a standard session when teammates run in plan mode.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/costs"},{"claim":"DeepLearning.AI’s short course “AI Agents in LangGraph” (announced 5 June 2024, taught by Harrison Chase of LangChain and Rotem Weiss of Tavily, with an introduction by Andrew Ng) builds a ReAct agent from scratch, then the same agent as a graph of nodes and conditional edges with saved state, streaming and human-in-the-loop edits. It predates LangGraph 1.0, so check current APIs against LangGraph’s own docs.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/ai-agents-in-langgraph/"}]],["What makes agents capable","#/map/t/models-what-makes-agents-capable",[{"claim":"Anthropic reached 49% on SWE-bench Verified with Claude 3.5 Sonnet using a minimal scaffold of a bash tool and an edit tool, and stated that SWE-bench evaluates the whole agent system, with scaffold optimisation greatly improving results around the same model (Jan 2025).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/swe-bench-sonnet"},{"claim":"SWE-agent (Yang et al., 2024) reached 12.5% pass@1 on SWE-bench with a custom agent-computer interface, far above the prior non-interactive state of the art.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.15793"},{"claim":"A May 2026 position paper (Zhang et al.) argues the agent harness is often a stronger determinant of long-horizon performance than the model it wraps, among frontier-capable models, and that harness-induced variance can exceed model-induced variance, including cases of model-ranking reversal.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.23950"},{"claim":"METR’s live time-horizons page lists measurements up to 8 May 2026 and warns that measurements above 16 hours are unreliable with its current task suite; it gives no updated doubling time, so the 7-month figure below is a March 2025 estimate.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://metr.org/time-horizons/"},{"claim":"METR (March 2025) found the length of tasks AI agents complete with 50% reliability doubling about every 7 months over six years; models then succeeded almost always on tasks under 4 human-minutes and under 10% of the time on tasks over about 4 hours.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://metr.org/blog/2025-03-19-measuring-ai-ability-to-complete-long-tasks/"},{"claim":"METR states time horizons measure task difficulty on self-contained, well-specified software, ML and cyber tasks, not how long agents act autonomously, and that measurements above about 16 hours are unreliable with its current suite (page updated May 2026).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://metr.org/time-horizons/"},{"claim":"On BrowseComp, Anthropic found token usage alone explained 80% of performance variance in its research agent (June 2025).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system"},{"claim":"Anthropic reported its multi-agent research system (Opus 4 lead, Sonnet 4 sub-agents) outperformed single-agent Opus 4 by 90.2% on its internal research eval.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system"},{"claim":"METR’s randomised trial of 16 experienced open-source developers on 246 issues found they took 19% longer with AI tools allowed, while forecasting a 24% speed-up and afterwards believing in a 20% speed-up (July 2025, early-2025 tools). METR now marks these results as out of date. Its 24 February 2026 update (57 developers, 800+ tasks, late-2025 tools) estimated -18% time for returning developers (interval -38% to +9%) and -4% for new ones (-15% to +9%), and METR calls that only very weak evidence because of selection effects.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/"}]],["Tools, MCP, structured output and RAG","#/map/t/models-tools-mcp-rag",[{"claim":"The current MCP specification revision, 2026-07-28, uses JSON-RPC 2.0 messages between hosts, clients and servers with stateless, self-contained requests; servers may offer resources, prompts and tools, clients may offer elicitation, and sampling and roots are deprecated in that revision.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28"},{"claim":"The same specification says tools represent arbitrary code execution, that tool descriptions such as annotations should be treated as untrusted unless from a trusted server, that hosts must obtain user consent before invoking a tool, and that MCP cannot enforce these principles at the protocol level.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28"},{"claim":"OpenAI’s documentation says Structured Outputs makes responses adhere to a supplied JSON Schema, while JSON mode only guarantees valid JSON; strict mode also applies to function calling, and some schema features are not supported.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/structured-outputs"},{"claim":"Anthropic’s Contextual Retrieval post (19 September 2024) reports that contextual embeddings cut the top-20-chunk retrieval failure rate by 35%, adding contextual BM25 by 49%, and adding reranking by 67%; it also says a knowledge base under about 200,000 tokens (about 500 pages) can simply go in the prompt.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval"}]],["Reasoning models, benchmarks and scaling","#/map/t/models-reasoning-and-scaling",[{"claim":"Kaplan et al., \"Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models\" (submitted 23 January 2020), found that loss scales as a power law with model size, dataset size and training compute, with some trends spanning more than seven orders of magnitude.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361"},{"claim":"Hoffmann et al., \"Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models\" (submitted 29 March 2022), found that training tokens should double with each doubling of model size; Chinchilla (70B parameters, four times Gopher’s data at the same compute) outperformed Gopher (280B) and reached 67.5% on MMLU.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556"},{"claim":"The DeepSeek-R1 paper (January 2025, later published in Nature volume 645) reports that reasoning behaviours such as self-reflection and verification emerged from reinforcement learning without human-annotated reasoning demonstrations.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948"},{"claim":"METR uplift update (24 February 2026): 57 developers, 143 repositories, 800+ tasks; late-2025 estimates of -18% time for returning developers (interval -38% to +9%) and -4% for new developers (-15% to +9%). METR calls it only very weak evidence: developers who did not want to work without AI opted out, 30% to 50% of developers said they held back some tasks they did not want to do without it, and concurrent agents broke time tracking. The July 2025 trial (16 developers, 246 tasks) found 19% slower, interval +2% to +39%.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/"},{"claim":"METR time-horizons page (updated 8 May 2026): measurements above 16 hours are unreliable with the current task suite, so a longer horizon than that is not measured; tasks are self-contained and well specified. The exponential trend fits better than linear or hyperbolic.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://metr.org/time-horizons/"},{"claim":"OpenAI, in a 2026 post titled Why SWE-bench Verified no longer measures frontier coding capabilities, stopped reporting SWE-bench Verified and recommends SWE-bench Pro. As summarised in secondary coverage (the OpenAI page returned 403 to this check, so the figures are not read from the primary source): an audit of the subset of problems models often failed (27.6% of the dataset) found at least 59.4% of the audited problems had flawed tests rejecting correct solutions, and GPT-5.2, Claude Opus 4.5 and Gemini 3 Flash Preview could reproduce some original fixes from memory. Separately, the SWE-bench Pro paper (September 2025) reports 23% for the top models of the time (Opus 4.1, GPT-5) against over 70% for Verified. That gap compares two benchmarks, not a like-for-like drop.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://openai.com/index/why-we-no-longer-evaluate-swe-bench-verified/"},{"claim":"Humanity’s Last Exam (2,500 questions) was built because LLMs already exceeded 90% accuracy on popular benchmarks such as MMLU.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14249"},{"claim":"Yang et al. (November 2023) showed that paraphrased or translated test items bypass n-gram decontamination, and that a 13B model trained on such variants matched GPT-4 on MMLU.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.04850"},{"claim":"METR (19 March 2025) estimated that the length of tasks frontier models complete with 50% success has doubled about every 7 months over six years, noted that the 2024–2025 trend looked faster, and cautioned that the estimate depends on task and methodology choices.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://metr.org/blog/2025-03-19-measuring-ai-ability-to-complete-long-tasks/"},{"claim":"OpenAI, \"Learning to reason with LLMs\" (12 September 2024): on the 2024 AIME, GPT-4o averaged 12% (1.8 of 15) and o1 74% with a single sample, 83% with consensus among 64 samples and 93% re-ranking 1,000 samples; o1 ranked in the 89th percentile on Codeforces, and its performance improved with both more reinforcement learning and more time spent thinking.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/"},{"claim":"The same post says recent frontier models do so well on MATH and GSM8K that these benchmarks no longer separate models, which is why it reported AIME instead.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/"},{"claim":"OpenAI’s simple-evals results table lists o1 at 75.7 on GPQA against 53.1 for gpt-4o-2024-08-06; the repository stopped adding new models in July 2025.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://github.com/openai/simple-evals"}]],["Prompt injection, cost and production evals","#/map/t/models-security-and-operations",[{"claim":"The OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications 2025 ranks prompt injection first (LLM01) and distinguishes direct injection through user input from indirect injection through external content such as websites or files.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/"},{"claim":"Simon Willison’s \"lethal trifecta\" (16 June 2025) is access to private data, exposure to untrusted content and the ability to communicate externally; he calls a guardrail that catches 95% of attacks a failing grade, and he coined the term prompt injection on 12 September 2022, after Riley Goodside’s demonstration.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/"},{"claim":"Anthropic’s Message Batches API processes requests asynchronously at 50% of standard cost, with most batches finishing in under an hour.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing"}]],["AI orchestrates, tools compute","#/map/t/craft-ai-orchestrates-tools-compute",[{"claim":"PAL (Gao et al., 2022), in which the model writes Python and an interpreter computes the answer, with Codex beat PaLM-540B using chain-of-thought on GSM8K by 15 points absolute top-1 accuracy.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10435"},{"claim":"Toolformer (Schick et al., 2023) learned to call a calculator, a Q&A system, two search engines, a translator and a calendar from a handful of demonstrations per API, and was often competitive with much larger models.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761"},{"claim":"GSM-Symbolic (2024) found all tested models drop when only the numbers in a maths question change, and adding one clause that seems relevant but does not bear on the answer cut performance by up to 65%.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.05229"},{"claim":"DeepSeek-R1 (2025) reports 79.8% pass@1 on AIME 2024 and 97.3% on MATH-500, with no tool or code interpreter described in the evaluation.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.12948v1"},{"claim":"On tau-bench (2024), state-of-the-art function-calling agents such as GPT-4o succeeded on under 50% of tasks, and pass^8 fell below 25% in the retail domain. These are 2024 models; re-measure on the model you use.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045"},{"claim":"Anthropic’s code execution tool runs Bash and Python in a sandboxed container so the model can do calculations, analyse data and create visualisations inside the API conversation.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/code-execution-tool"}]],["Core values that last","#/map/t/craft-core-values-that-last",[{"claim":"Anthropic’s agent-building guidance advises finding the simplest solution possible and increasing complexity only when needed, and distinguishes predefined workflows from model-directed agents.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents"}]],["The engineer in the AI era","#/map/t/craft-engineer-in-the-ai-era",[{"claim":"A 2025 METR randomised controlled trial with 16 experienced open-source developers on 246 tasks found AI tools increased completion time by 19%, while the developers forecast a 24% speedup beforehand and estimated 20% afterwards.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.09089"},{"claim":"The 2024 DORA report associates a 25% increase in AI adoption with a 1.5% decrease in delivery throughput and a 7.2% reduction in delivery stability, alongside gains in documentation quality, code quality and review speed.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/announcing-the-2024-dora-report"},{"claim":"The 2025 DORA report, unlike 2024’s, found a positive relationship between AI adoption and software delivery throughput, while AI adoption still had a negative relationship with delivery stability.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/announcing-the-2025-dora-report"},{"claim":"METR, Uplift update (24 February 2026): 57 developers (10 returning, 47 new), 143 repositories, 800+ tasks. Late-2025 estimates were -18% time for returning developers (interval -38% to +9%) and -4% for new developers (-15% to +9%). METR calls this only very weak evidence: developers unwilling to work without AI opted out, 30% to 50% of developers said they held back some tasks they did not want to do without it, and concurrent agents broke time tracking. It also says developers are likely more sped up in early 2026. It does not show a reliable speed-up number.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/"},{"claim":"He et al. (CMU, MSR 2026): across 806 GitHub repos that adopted Cursor and 1,380 matched controls (Jan 2024 to Aug 2025), lines added rose 281.3% in the first month and 48.4% in the second, then the effect faded, while static-analysis warnings rose about 30% and code complexity about 42% and stayed up. Observational, open-source only.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.04427"},{"claim":"DORA 2025 (about 5,000 respondents plus interviews) describes AI as an amplifier: teams with strong foundations (testing, version control, fast feedback, platforms) gain, and weak ones see instability. DORA is a correlational survey and does not prove cause.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/announcing-the-2025-dora-report"}]],["Verification as the job","#/map/t/craft-verification-as-the-job",[{"claim":"Codex (Chen et al., 2021) solved 28.8% of HumanEval problems with one sample and 70.2% with 100 samples per problem, measuring functional correctness with unit tests.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.03374"},{"claim":"EvalPlus (2023) extended HumanEval’s tests 80 times, which reduced reported pass@k of code models by up to 19.3 to 28.9%.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.01210"},{"claim":"OpenAI, \"Learning to reason with LLMs\" (12 September 2024): a model trained from o1 scored 213 points at the 2024 IOI by selecting submissions with public test cases, model-generated test cases and a learned scoring function; submitting at random would have averaged 156 points.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/"},{"claim":"The OpenAI o1 System Card (December 2024) described SWE-bench Verified as a human-validated 500-task subset and reported o1 at 40.9% pass@1. Since then OpenAI has stopped reporting the benchmark: per its 2026 post as summarised in secondary coverage (the page itself returned 403 here), an audit of a subset of problems models often failed found at least 59.4% had flawed tests that reject correct solutions, and frontier models could reproduce some original fixes from memory. So even a human-validated test suite can be wrong.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://openai.com/index/why-we-no-longer-evaluate-swe-bench-verified/"},{"claim":"Veracode (vendor report, 2025): 45% of code samples from 100+ models failed security tests, and cross-site scripting was defended in only 14% of relevant samples. It is a security vendor’s own measurement.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.veracode.com/blog/genai-code-security-report/"}]],["Best practice that still pays","#/map/t/craft-best-practice-now",[{"claim":"Google’s engineering practices guide recommends small CLs, saying they are reviewed more quickly and thoroughly, are less likely to introduce bugs, and are easier to roll back.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/small-cls.html"}]],["Memory, allocation and garbage collection","#/map/t/craft-memory-and-gc",[{"claim":"Unity uses the Boehm-Demers-Weiser garbage collector and enables incremental garbage collection by default, spreading collection over multiple frames; the web platform does not support incremental mode, and heavy reference changes can force a full collection.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/performance-incremental-garbage-collection.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s RefCounted frees itself when no references remain, while a plain Object must be freed manually with free(); RefCounted cycles are not freed automatically and weakref() can break them.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_refcounted.html"}]],["Performance: profile, budget, lay out data","#/map/t/craft-performance",[{"claim":"Unity’s documentation says ProfilerMarker’s Begin and End methods carry ConditionalAttribute and are compiled away, with zero overhead, in non-development (release) builds.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Unity.Profiling.ProfilerMarker.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s Performance class exposes the values shown in the editor Debugger’s Monitor tab through get_monitor(), including TIME_PROCESS, TIME_PHYSICS_PROCESS, OBJECT_COUNT and RENDER_TOTAL_DRAW_CALLS_IN_FRAME, and supports add_custom_monitor().","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_performance.html"},{"claim":"Mike Acton’s CppCon 2014 talk \"Data-Oriented Design and C++\" argues that the purpose of code is to transform data and that design should start from the data and the hardware.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rX0ItVEVjHc"}]],["Design patterns in games","#/map/t/craft-design-patterns",[{"claim":"Nystrom’s Game Programming Patterns groups its chapters as Design Patterns Revisited (command, flyweight, observer, prototype, singleton, state), Sequencing (double buffer, game loop, update method), Behavioral (bytecode, subclass sandbox, type object), Decoupling (component, event queue, service locator) and Optimization (data locality, dirty flag, object pool, spatial partition).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gameprogrammingpatterns.com/contents.html"}]],["Clean code in the AI era","#/map/t/craft-clean-code-ai-era",[{"claim":"John Ousterhout and Robert C. Martin published a written discussion comparing A Philosophy of Software Design and Clean Code, held between September 2024 and February 2025, covering method length, comments and test-driven development; they agreed unit tests are essential and left their disagreement on test-driven development unresolved.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/johnousterhout/aposd-vs-clean-code"},{"claim":"GitClear’s \"AI Copilot Code Quality\" report (February 2025, 211 million changed lines from 2020 to 2024) found that 2024 was the first year copy/pasted lines exceeded moved lines, that moved lines fell from 25% of changed lines in 2021 to under 10% in 2024, and that blocks of five or more duplicated lines became eight times as frequent during 2024.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://gitclear-public.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/GitClear-AI-Copilot-Code-Quality-2025.pdf"}]],["Over-defensive code","#/map/t/craft-over-defensive-code",[{"claim":"Unity’s Object documentation states that destroyed (detached) objects compare equal to null through Unity’s overloaded == operator, while ReferenceEquals returns false, and that the ?. and ?? operators are not supported with Unity Objects because they cannot be overridden to treat detached objects as null.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Object.html"}]],["What matters now","#/map/t/craft-what-matters-now",[{"claim":"Anthropic’s engineering post Building effective agents, published 19 December 2024, recommends finding the simplest solution possible and increasing complexity only when needed.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents"}]],["Teaching agents to avoid pitfalls","#/map/t/craft-teaching-agents",[{"claim":"Claude Code’s memory documentation says CLAUDE.md files and auto memory are treated as context, not enforced configuration, and recommends a PreToolUse hook to block an action regardless of what Claude decides.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory"},{"claim":"Claude Code’s memory documentation recommends keeping each CLAUDE.md under 200 lines, says longer files reduce adherence, and supports @path imports and path-scoped rules in .claude/rules.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory"},{"claim":"Claude Code hooks documentation: a PreToolUse hook that exits with code 2 blocks the tool call; hooks can be configured in ~/.claude/settings.json, a project’s .claude/settings.json or .claude/settings.local.json, managed policy, plugins, and skill or subagent frontmatter.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks"},{"claim":"AGENTS.md is an open format stewarded by the Agentic AI Foundation under the Linux Foundation, used by over 60,000 open-source projects per its site, where the nearest AGENTS.md in a directory tree takes precedence.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://agents.md/"},{"claim":"Cursor stores project rules as .mdc files in .cursor/rules, applied always, by agent judgement from a description, by file pattern, or manually by @-mention, and also supports AGENTS.md.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://cursor.com/docs/context/rules"}]],["Tools that work with AI","#/map/t/craft-tools-that-work-with-ai",[{"claim":"Unity Asset Serialization Mode offers Mixed, Force Binary and Force Text, and Force Text is the default.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.6/Documentation/Manual/class-EditorManager.html"},{"claim":"A Unity .meta file holds the asset’s unique ID and all its import settings, and must stay with the asset file it relates to.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.6/Documentation/Manual/AssetMetadata.html"},{"claim":"Epic’s Perforce guide states .uasset files are binary and cannot be merged in a text-based tool.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/using-perforce-as-source-control-for-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Blender -b runs without its interface, -P runs a Python script, and --python-exit-code sets a non-zero exit code when a Python exception is raised.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/blender/blender/blob/v5.2.2/source/creator/creator_args.cc"},{"claim":"The MCP specification (revision 2026-07-28) uses stateless JSON-RPC requests; servers offer resources, prompts and tools, and elicitation is the only client feature; it says tools represent arbitrary code execution and that hosts must obtain explicit user consent before invoking any tool.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28"},{"claim":"The mcp-for-blender community server exposes an execute_blender_code tool that runs arbitrary Python, and its README says to save your work before using it.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/ahujasid/mcp-for-blender"}]],["Source control for games","#/map/t/craft-source-control-for-games",[{"claim":"git lfs track with --lockable adds the lockable attribute; Git LFS makes lockable files read-only locally until locked with git lfs lock, and on push verifies you are not modifying a file another user locked; servers without the locking API give a warning instead.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/wiki/File-Locking"},{"claim":"GitHub’s Git LFS per-file limit is 2 GB on Free and Pro, 4 GB on Team and 5 GB on Enterprise Cloud.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/working-with-files/managing-large-files/about-git-large-file-storage"},{"claim":"Perforce P4 (Helix Core) is free for up to 5 users and 20 workspaces, with file locking and streams.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.perforce.com/products/helix-core/free-version-control"},{"claim":"Unity Version Control, formerly Plastic SCM, works with any engine including Unreal and offers file locking, Smart Locks and the Gluon client.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/solutions/version-control"},{"claim":"Since 1 March 2026 the cloud-hosted Unity Version Control charges nothing for seats, and the first 25 GB of storage per organisation each month is free, measured as a monthly average in GB instead of GB-hours; on-premises terms may differ.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://support.unity.com/hc/en-us/articles/34748492914964-Understanding-Unity-DevOps-charges"},{"claim":"Godot’s docs say to exclude the .godot/ folder and *.translation files, and to use core.autocrlf input on Windows; generated metadata adds a .gitattributes that enforces LF.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/best_practices/version_control_systems.html"},{"claim":"UnityYAMLMerge merges scene and prefab files semantically; it ships with the Editor, under Data/Tools on Windows and Contents/Helpers on macOS, and the manual gives a Git mergetool configuration for it.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.6/Documentation/Manual/SmartMerge.html"},{"claim":"Unreal’s DerivedDataCache is not committed to version control and can be regenerated from the .uasset files.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/using-derived-data-cache-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Since Godot 4.4 scripts and shaders get .uid files beside them, which must be committed; scenes and resources store their UID in the file header and imported assets in their .import file.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://godotengine.org/article/uid-changes-coming-to-godot-4-4/"}]],["Gameplay math","#/map/t/craft-gameplay-math",[{"claim":"Godot’s transform guide says that Euler angles have no unique construction of an orientation, that interpolating them suffers from gimbal lock, that quaternions interpolate with slerp, and that quaternions used repeatedly must eventually be normalised.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/3d/using_transforms.html"},{"claim":"Unity’s Quaternion.Slerp(a, b, t) interpolates spherically between unit quaternions and clamps t to the range 0 to 1.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Quaternion.Slerp.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s vector maths tutorial covers the dot and cross products and their geometric meaning.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/math/vector_math.html"}]],["The game loop and fixed timestep","#/map/t/craft-game-loop-timestep",[{"claim":"Glenn Fiedler’s \"Fix Your Timestep\" recommends a fixed simulation step with an accumulator, interpolation of rendering between the previous and current state, and warns about the spiral of death when simulation is slower than the time it represents.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://gafferongames.com/post/fix_your_timestep/"},{"claim":"Unity’s Time project settings define Fixed Timestep as the frame-rate independent interval for physics calculations and FixedUpdate events, and Maximum Allowed Timestep as a cap on the worst case when the frame rate is low.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/class-TimeManager.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s Engine class exposes physics_ticks_per_second and max_physics_steps_per_frame, which set the physics step rate and the catch-up cap.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_engine.html"}]],["Entities, scenes and ECS","#/map/t/craft-entities-and-scenes",[{"claim":"Godot’s scene organisation best practices advise designing scenes with no hard dependencies, warn that hard-coded node paths stop finding their targets when a scene is reused, and say a signal connection should respond to behaviour, not start it.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/best_practices/scene_organization.html"},{"claim":"Unity’s Entities documentation defines an entity as a unique identifier, a component as data about the entity, and a system as the logic that processes entity data, and lists structural changes as something that affects performance.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.entities@1.3/manual/concepts-intro.html"},{"claim":"Robert Nystrom’s Game Programming Patterns chapter \"Component\" splits a large object into separate input, physics and graphics components to reduce coupling and avoid inheritance, and points to its Data Locality chapter for the cache reasons behind packed data.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://gameprogrammingpatterns.com/component.html"}]],["Physics and collision","#/map/t/craft-physics-and-collision",[{"claim":"Unity’s Rigidbody.collisionDetectionMode has Discrete (default), Continuous, ContinuousDynamic and ContinuousSpeculative modes. The docs advise continuous detection to stop fast bodies passing through others, note the performance cost, and say continuous works only with sphere, capsule and box colliders.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Rigidbody-collisionDetectionMode.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s CharacterBody2D is for bodies controlled through code. move_and_slide() slides along surfaces, and movement should be done in _physics_process, not by setting position directly.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/physics/using_character_body_2d.html"},{"claim":"Maddy Thorson’s article on Celeste and TowerFall physics explains how movement and collision between Actors and Solids are handled in both games, as a reference for code-driven character movement.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.maddymakesgames.com/articles/celeste_and_towerfall_physics/index.html"}]],["Save systems","#/map/t/craft-save-systems",[{"claim":"Godot maps user:// to a per-user folder that differs per platform (for example %APPDATA%\\Godot\\app_userdata\\[project_name] on Windows), and on mobile the folder is not accessible to other applications.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/io/data_paths.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s DirAccess.rename overwrites an existing destination if it is not access-protected, and the docs make no atomicity promise.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_diraccess.html"},{"claim":"POSIX rename(): if the destination exists it is removed and the source renamed, and the destination name stays visible throughout, referring to either the old or the new file.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/rename.html"},{"claim":"Windows MoveFileEx replaces the contents of an existing destination only when MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING is set, and the page states no atomicity guarantee.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-movefileexw"},{"claim":"Unity’s Application.persistentDataPath returns a directory path for data files that persists between runs and updates, and differs per platform.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Application-persistentDataPath.html"},{"claim":".NET’s File.Replace replaces the contents of a destination file with a source file and creates a backup of the replaced file (pass null to skip it). The destination must exist, or FileNotFoundException is thrown, and source and destination on different volumes raise an exception.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.io.file.replace"}]],["Measuring whether AI helps your team","#/map/t/craft-measuring-ai-uplift",[{"claim":"METR randomised trial (Becker, Rush, Barnes, Rein; July 2025): 16 experienced open-source developers, 246 tasks in mature repositories; with AI allowed, tasks took 19% longer (interval +2% to +39% in the February 2026 revision). Developers forecast 24% faster and afterwards believed 20% faster. The authors say experimental artifacts cannot be entirely ruled out.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.09089"},{"claim":"METR, \"Uplift update\" (24 February 2026): a redesigned study of 57 developers, 143 repositories and 800+ tasks estimated returning developers 18% faster (interval -38% to +9% in time) and new developers 4% faster (-15% to +9%). METR calls it only very weak evidence because of selection (developers unwilling to work without AI opted out; 30 to 50% of developers said they held back some tasks they did not want to do without AI) and broken time tracking with concurrent agents.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/"},{"claim":"Cui et al., three field experiments at Microsoft, Accenture and a Fortune 100 firm (4,867 developers, Copilot, 2 to 8 months): +26.08% completed tasks (SE 10.3%; the paper counts these as weekly pull requests), +13.55% commits (SE 10.0%, not clearly different from zero), +38.38% compiles (SE 12.55%); less experienced developers adopted more and gained more. Quality and agentic tools were not measured.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/draft_copilot_experiments.pdf"},{"claim":"Peng et al. (February 2023): in a controlled experiment on one task, writing an HTTP server in JavaScript, the Copilot group finished 55.8% faster.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06590"},{"claim":"He et al., \"Speed at the Cost of Quality\" (November 2025, MSR 2026): 806 GitHub repositories adopting Cursor against 1,380 matched controls, January 2024 to August 2025. Lines added +281.3% in month one and +48.4% in month two, then faded; static-analysis warnings rose about 30% and code complexity about 42%, both persisting. Observational, open source only, adoption inferred from a committed Cursor config file.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.04427"},{"claim":"DORA 2024 (survey, association not cause): a 25% rise in AI adoption went with -1.5% delivery throughput and -7.2% delivery stability. DORA 2025 (about 5,000 respondents plus interviews): throughput now positively related to AI use, stability still negatively related; AI described as an amplifier of existing team capabilities.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/announcing-the-2025-dora-report"},{"claim":"Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2025 (33,662 people answered the AI-use question; the survey had 49,000+ responses in all): 84% use or plan to use AI tools; 66% say AI answers are \"almost right, but not quite\"; 45.2% say debugging AI-generated code takes longer. Self-reported, self-selected respondents.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/ai"}]],["Security review of generated code","#/map/t/craft-security-review-of-generated-code",[{"claim":"Pearce et al., \"Asleep at the Keyboard?\" (August 2021): 89 scenarios based on the MITRE top-25 weaknesses, 1,689 programs generated by Copilot; about 40% contained vulnerabilities. Old model; not a rate for current tools.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.09293"},{"claim":"Perry, Srivastava, Kumar, Boneh, \"Do Users Write More Insecure Code with AI Assistants?\" (November 2022, v3 December 2023): 47 participants (33 with an assistant, 14 control, 66% undergraduates), five security tasks, Codex; the assistant group was less secure on most tasks (for example 3% against 21% secure for message signing, 12% against 29% for file access; on the SQL task 36% of the assistant group wrote injectable code against 7% of the control group) and more likely to rate insecure answers as secure. Two-hour lab study with an old model.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.03622"},{"claim":"Veracode, \"2025 GenAI Code Security Report\" (a security vendor’s own test, vendor claim): more than 100 models on Java, JavaScript, Python and C#; 45% of samples failed security tests (Java 72%, C# 45%, JavaScript 43%, Python 38%); cross-site scripting defended in only 14% of relevant samples; newer and larger models did not improve security. Task count and prompt design not stated on the pages read.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://www.veracode.com/blog/genai-code-security-report/"}]],["Cost and latency budget for AI features","#/map/t/craft-ai-cost-latency-budget",[{"claim":"Anthropic pricing page, read 30 September 2026: Claude Sonnet 5.5 $2 per million input tokens and $10 output; Haiku 4.5 $1 and $5; Opus 5.5 $4 and $20; Fable 5.1 $10 and $50. Cache multipliers on base input: 5-minute write 1.25x, 1-hour write 2x, read 0.1x (0.05x on Opus 5.5, 0.025x on Fable 5.1). Vendor prices; check the page again before relying on them.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"},{"claim":"Anthropic pricing page, 30 September 2026: the Batch API gives a 50% discount on input and output tokens for asynchronous requests; Claude 4.7 and later models use a tokenizer that produces about 30% more tokens for the same text; Sonnet 5’s $2/$10 launch price became the standard price and the planned rise to $3/$15 on 1 September 2026 was cancelled.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"},{"claim":"METR time-horizons page (updated 8 May 2026): the exponential trend fits better than linear or hyperbolic, and measurements above 16 hours are unreliable with the current task suite; tasks are self-contained and well specified, so this is not a measure of autonomy on messy real work.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://metr.org/time-horizons/"}]],["CPU time, caches and data layout","#/map/t/craft-cpu-cache-and-data-layout",[{"claim":"Unity ECS stores entities of one archetype in 16 KiB chunks, laying each component type out as its own array inside the chunk.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.entities@1.3/manual/concepts-archetypes.html"},{"claim":"Unity’s ObjectPool<T> takes createFunc, actionOnGet, actionOnRelease, actionOnDestroy, collectionCheck, defaultCapacity and maxSize; it stores items in a stack so the objects are not necessarily contiguous in memory, and it is not safe to call from background threads.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Pool.ObjectPool_1.html"},{"claim":"Burst compiles a subset of C# (High-Performance C#) with LLVM; branches inside a loop can stop it vectorising, and Loop.ExpectVectorized (behind UNITY_BURST_EXPERIMENTAL_LOOP_INTRINSICS) makes the compiler report when a loop is not vectorised.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.burst@1.8/manual/optimization-loop-vectorization.html"},{"claim":"The Godot debugger Profiler does not currently support C# scripts; the documentation points to Rider or dotTrace with the Godot plugins instead.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/debug/the_profiler.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s WorkerThreadPool.add_group_task(action, elements, tasks_needed = -1, high_priority = false, description = \"\") runs a Callable once per element on pooled worker threads; the scene tree is not thread-safe.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_workerthreadpool.html"},{"claim":"Unreal’s Tasks System launches work with UE::Tasks::Launch, supports prerequisites, nested tasks and pipes, and shares its scheduler and worker threads with the older TaskGraph.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/tasks-systems-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Cache lines are 64 bytes on mainstream processors; Drepper quotes Intel’s figures for a Pentium M of about 3 cycles for L1d, 14 for L2 and 240 for main memory. Current chips are faster in absolute terms but the ratios are of the same order.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://lwn.net/Articles/252125/"},{"claim":"Unity’s Deep Profile setting instruments all function calls, is resource-intensive and makes the application run significantly slower; Unity exposes the cache line size as JobsUtility.CacheLineSize.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/profiler-deep-profiling.html"}]],["GPU cost: draw calls, overdraw, shaders and LOD","#/map/t/craft-gpu-rendering-cost",[{"claim":"Unreal’s stat unit shows Frame, Game, Draw, GPU, RHIT and DynRes times; the documentation says frame time is likely bottlenecked by the game thread or by the rendering thread when it is about equal to that thread’s time.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/stat-commands-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Unreal’s Dynamic Resolution adjusts the primary screen percentage from the previous frames’ GPU workload; r.DynamicRes.MinScreenPercentage defaults to 50, MaxScreenPercentage to 100 and FrameTimeBudget to 33.3 ms; it is supported on current consoles and on PC with DX12 or Vulkan.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/dynamic-resolution-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Unity’s manual says the SRP Batcher reduces render-state changes between draw calls rather than the number of draw calls; it works in URP and HDRP and not in the Built-in Render Pipeline.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/SRPBatcher.html"},{"claim":"Unity’s dynamic batching (not supported in HDRP) limits meshes to 300 vertices and 900 vertex attributes and the manual says it is no longer recommended because the CPU overhead can exceed the cost of a draw call; a static batch buffer holds up to 64,000 vertices; the manual says to use static batching when you want static meshes culled individually.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/DrawCallBatching.html"},{"claim":"Unity’s GPU instancing draws many GameObjects that share a mesh and material in one draw call; Skinned Mesh Renderers are not supported, and with URP or HDRP custom shaders need the SRP Batcher disabled or the shader made incompatible with it.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/GPUInstancing.html"},{"claim":"Graphics.RenderMeshIndirect renders instances using draw arguments read from a graphics buffer and only works on platforms that support compute shaders.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Graphics.RenderMeshIndirect.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s MultiMesh draws thousands of instances in one draw call, and the manual states no screen or frustum culling is possible for individual instances: the whole MultiMesh is culled with one bounding box.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/performance/using_multimesh.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s Visual Profiler graphs rendering CPU time and GPU time by category, and shows rendering CPU time on the left and GPU time on the right.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/debug/debugger_panel.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s Viewport scaling_3d_scale below 1.0 renders 3D at lower resolution; modes include bilinear, FSR 1.0, FSR 2.2 and MetalFX; AMD’s recommended FSR presets are 0.77, 0.67, 0.59 and 0.5.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_viewport.html"},{"claim":"Unity recommends ASTC on iOS and on Android devices with OpenGL ES 3.1 or Vulkan, ranging from 8 bits per pixel (4 by 4) to 0.89 bits per pixel (12 by 12); on desktop DXT1 at 4 bits per pixel and BC7 or DXT5 at 8.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/texture-choose-format-by-platform.html"},{"claim":"RenderDoc is a graphics debugger for Vulkan, D3D11, D3D12, OpenGL and OpenGL ES on Windows, Linux, Android and Nintendo Switch.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://renderdoc.org/docs/index.html"}]],["Mobile GPUs, battery and heat","#/map/t/craft-mobile-gpu-and-thermals",[{"claim":"Android’s Thermal API reports status levels NONE, LIGHT, MODERATE, SEVERE, CRITICAL, EMERGENCY and SHUTDOWN. getCurrentThermalStatus() needs API level 29 and getThermalHeadroom(int forecastSeconds) needs API level 30. In the NDK, AThermal_acquireManager and AThermal_getCurrentThermalStatus need API level 30 and AThermal_getThermalHeadroom needs API level 31. Headroom of 0.0 means no throttling and 1.0 means severe throttling. Google advises calling headroom no more often than every 10 seconds and warns some devices report NONE even when throttled.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.android.com/games/optimize/adpf/thermal"},{"claim":"The Android Frame Pacing library (Swappy) works with OpenGL ES and Vulkan and chooses swap intervals to match the display: on a 60 Hz device 60, 30 or 20 fps; on 60 plus 90 Hz, 90, 60, 45 or 30 fps. Unity has it built in as Optimized Frame Pacing.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.android.com/games/sdk/frame-pacing"},{"claim":"Unity’s Application.targetFrameRate defaults to -1. On Android and iOS, QualitySettings.vSyncCount values above 0 are ignored and targetFrameRate is the control; with vSyncCount 0 and targetFrameRate -1 the game renders at a fixed 30 fps; and the rate is rounded down to a value that divides the display refresh rate. On desktop and web, vSyncCount takes priority when it is not 0.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Application-targetFrameRate.html"},{"claim":"Google Play lists ETC2 on more than 95 percent of Android devices (guaranteed with OpenGL ES 3.0 and Vulkan) and ASTC on more than 80 percent, and delivers per-format asset packs through texture compression format targeting.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.android.com/guide/playcore/asset-delivery/texture-compression"},{"claim":"ASTC always compresses to 128-bit blocks; 4x4 is 8.00 bits per texel, 5x5 5.12, 8x8 2.00, 10x10 1.28 and 12x12 0.89. The LDR, HDR and full profiles differ in colour range and 3D support.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://github.com/ARM-software/astc-encoder/blob/main/Docs/FormatOverview.md"},{"claim":"Arm describes Mali tiles as 16 by 16 pixels, kept in fast on-chip memory, with only the tile’s colour written back to memory at the end. It gives roughly 120 mW per GB/s for external DRAM access and says on-chip memory is about an order of magnitude cheaper in energy. This is a 2014 post about older hardware; the figures could not be re-checked against the raw page and are orders of magnitude only.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.arm.com/community/arm-community-blogs/b/mobile-graphics-and-gaming-blog/posts/the-mali-gpu-an-abstract-machine-part-2---tile-based-rendering"},{"claim":"Qualcomm’s Adreno mobile best practices advise: minimise render passes, use CLEAR or DONT_CARE load ops, avoid framebuffer fetch (it is listed among the cases that disable LRZ for the current draw), use lazily allocated memory for MSAA and depth attachments, use ASTC and strict half precision (which it says can be twice as power-efficient and deliver twice the performance), and render at reduced resolution then upscale.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.qualcomm.com/bundle/publicresource/80-78185-2/topics/mobile_best_practices.md"},{"claim":"Imagination’s PowerVR guidance says TBDR hardware removes occluded fragments before shading, advises avoiding discard and alpha test, and recommends the draw order opaque, alpha-tested, then blended.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.imgtec.com/starter-guides/powervr-architecture/html/topics/rules/do-not-use-discard.html"},{"claim":"Unity’s Adaptive Performance package exposes ThermalStatus.ThermalMetrics with WarningLevel (NoWarning, ThrottlingImminent, Throttling), TemperatureLevel in 0 to 1 and TemperatureTrend in -1 to 1.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.adaptiveperformance@5.1/api/UnityEngine.AdaptivePerformance.ThermalMetrics.html"},{"claim":"Godot’s RenderingServer.viewport_get_measured_render_time_gpu returns GPU time in milliseconds, needs viewport_set_measure_render_time enabled first, and reflects GPU load even when the frame rate is capped; GPUs may lower clocks at low utilisation, which raises the reported time.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/godotengine/godot/master/doc/classes/RenderingServer.xml"},{"claim":"The Android Dynamic Performance Framework (ADPF) groups the Thermal API with performance hint sessions and a Game Mode API for games.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.android.com/games/optimize/adpf"}]],["Memory budgets, loading and streaming","#/map/t/craft-memory-loading-and-streaming",[{"claim":"Unity's Application.backgroundLoadingPriority limits main-thread time for asset integration to 2 ms (Low), 4 ms (BelowNormal, the default on most platforms), 10 ms (Normal) or 50 ms (High) per frame; it applies only in a built player.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Application-backgroundLoadingPriority.html"},{"claim":"In Addressables an AssetBundle is unloaded only when its reference count reaches zero, and you cannot unload part of a bundle: one referenced asset keeps the whole bundle in memory.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.addressables@2.3/manual/MemoryManagement.html"},{"claim":"Godot 4.4 and later precompile pipelines at load time using ubershaders. It works with the Forward+ and Mobile renderers, not Compatibility.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/performance/pipeline_compilations.html"},{"claim":"Unity's GraphicsStateCollection API traces pipeline states in development builds only, is marked experimental, and should be traced per graphics API and platform.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/shader-pso-trace.html"},{"claim":"Epic's PSO caching documentation says generating a new PSO on demand can take 100 ms or more, and ray tracing PSOs were not supported by the cache in Unreal Engine 5.0.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/optimizing-rendering-with-pso-caches-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Microsoft's Xbox DirectStorage documentation sets a design goal of 50,000 requests per second for 5 to 10 percent of one CPU core, a 2.0 GB/s minimum raw throughput guarantee over a 250 ms window, and advises submitting all requests rather than holding them back.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/features/console/storage/directstorage/directstorage-overview?view=gdk-2604"},{"claim":"DirectStorage 1.1 on Windows adds GPU decompression of GDeflate data, compressed in independent 64 KiB tiles so that CPU threads or GPU thread groups work in parallel.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://devblogs.microsoft.com/directx/directstorage-1-1-now-available/"},{"claim":"Google Play limits compressed download sizes: individual asset packs 1.5 GB, install-time modules and packs together 4 GB, and on-demand or fast-follow packs together 30 GB. Limits have changed before; recheck in Play Console.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859372?hl=en"},{"claim":"App Store Connect allows a maximum uncompressed iOS app size of 4 GB (iOS 9 and later) and a limit of 500 MB for the total of all executable __TEXT sections (iOS 9 and later). Older limits apply to apps that still target iOS 8 or earlier.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/reference/app-uploads/maximum-build-file-sizes/"},{"claim":"Unity's manual says mipmaps increase a texture's size by 33 percent on disk and in memory, and offers Mipmap Streaming to control which levels load at runtime.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/texture-mipmaps-introduction.html"},{"claim":"Insomniac's Mike Fitzgerald said the PS5's SSD and I/O let the studio get a texture or resource into memory almost instantly for Ratchet and Clank: Rift Apart, and described using memory more freely as a result (PlayStation Blog).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.playstation.com/en-au/editorial/how-ps5-helped-make-ratchet-and-clank-rift-apart-possible/"}]],["Web performance: load fast, stay responsive","#/map/t/web-performance-basics",[{"claim":"web.dev's Core Web Vitals guidance sets the good thresholds at LCP 2.5 seconds or less, INP 200 milliseconds or less and CLS 0.1 or less, assessed at the 75th percentile of page loads and segmented by mobile and desktop. INP replaced FID as a stable Core Web Vital in 2024.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://web.dev/articles/vitals"},{"claim":"INP bands are good at 200 ms or less, needs improvement from 201 to 500 ms, poor above 500 ms; LCP is poor above 4.0 seconds; CLS is poor above 0.25.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://web.dev/articles/inp"},{"claim":"web.dev defines a long task as work over 50 ms and recommends scheduler.yield() with a setTimeout fallback, yielding about every 50 ms of work.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://web.dev/articles/optimize-long-tasks"},{"claim":"Lighthouse 10 weights its performance score as Total Blocking Time 30 percent, LCP 25, CLS 25, First Contentful Paint 10 and Speed Index 10.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.chrome.com/docs/lighthouse/performance/performance-scoring"},{"claim":"AppLovin requires a playable to be a single HTML file of 5 MB or smaller with resources embedded and external network calls prohibited; Unity Ads requires one inlined, minified index.html under 5 MB, MRAID 3.0 compliance, and no network requests except permitted analytics.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://support.applovin.com/en/growth/promoting-your-apps/creatives/best-practices-and-guidelines"},{"claim":"Unity's creative specifications page states the playable file limits for Unity Ads (single HTML, under 5 MB, MRAID 3.0).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity.com/en-us/user-acquisition/creatives/creative-specifications"},{"claim":"Meta's own help page allows a playable as a single HTML5 file of up to 5 MB, or a ZIP of up to 5 MB with at most 100 files. Google Ads requires a ZIP of up to 5 MB and 512 files. Limits for other networks (Mintegral, TikTok, Liftoff, Moloco) were not confirmed on a primary page for this topic; open each network's own spec.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/412951382532338"},{"claim":"Google Ads' playable (HTML5) requirements give a ZIP maximum of 5 MB with up to 512 files.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/9981650"},{"claim":"Godot's web export documentation says the .wasm file compresses to about a quarter of its size with gzip, recommends server compression (Brotli pre-compression where supported), and says single-threaded export is the default since Godot 4.3. Threads need the Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy and Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy headers. Godot 4 on the web supports only WebGL 2.0.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/export/exporting_for_web.html"},{"claim":"Unity's WebGL deployment manual offers Gzip (default), Brotli and Disabled compression, requires matching Content-Encoding headers, and requires Content-Type application/wasm for WebAssembly streaming compilation. A Decompression Fallback embeds a JavaScript decompressor at the cost of a larger loader, and the manual states it disables WebAssembly streaming compilation.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/webgl-deploying.html"},{"claim":"MDN's caching guide recommends Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable for cache-busted (versioned) subresources and no-cache with an ETag for HTML documents.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Caching"},{"claim":"The V8 team's \"Cost of JavaScript in 2019\" reports a median phone taking 3 to 4 times as long as a high-end phone to execute JavaScript and low-end phones over 6 times, and advises bundles under 50 to 100 kB; the figures are from 2019 and the ratio, not the absolute numbers, is the lesson.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://v8.dev/blog/cost-of-javascript-2019"}]],["Audio clock, input latency and calibration","#/map/t/craft-audio-clock-and-input-latency",[{"claim":"Godot 4 documents two ways to sync gameplay to audio: a system clock method that the page recommends for songs of a few minutes and that drifts on long ones because the sound hardware clock is never exactly in sync with the system clock, and a sound-hardware method (playback position plus get_time_since_last_mix minus get_output_latency) that the page calls less precise and that suits songs of any length, with a warning to discard a value that decreases.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/audio/sync_with_audio.html"},{"claim":"Godot ProjectSettings lists audio/driver/mix_rate with default 44100 and audio/driver/output_latency with default 15 (milliseconds).","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_projectsettings.html"},{"claim":"Godot InputEvent documents one property, device, and no timestamp; Input.use_accumulated_input is enabled by default and merges similar events into one batch per rendered frame.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_inputevent.html"},{"claim":"Unity Input System: InputEvent.time is on the same timeline as Time.realtimeSinceStartup; the default update mode processes input each frame before MonoBehaviour.Update.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.inputsystem@1.11/manual/Events.html"},{"claim":"Android documents low-latency audio guidance with OboeTester figures: round-trip latency of 20 ms following all recommendations, 205 ms when the performance mode is not low latency, 62 ms with no output callback on a non-MMAP path, 53 ms with the buffer at maximum (results vary greatly between devices); recommended buffer size is twice the burst size.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.android.com/games/sdk/oboe/low-latency-audio"},{"claim":"Apple documents that setPreferredIOBufferDuration is a request, to be checked against ioBufferDuration, with a typical maximum of 0.093 s (4,096 frames at 44.1 kHz) and a minimum of at least 0.005 s (256 frames) that may be lower on some hardware.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/avfaudio/avaudiosession/setpreferrediobufferduration(_:)"},{"claim":"ITU-R BT.1359-1 gives detectability thresholds for broadcast audio-video timing of about +45 ms (audio lead) to -125 ms (audio lag), and acceptability thresholds of about +90 ms to -185 ms.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://www.itu.int/dms_pubrec/itu-r/rec/bt/R-REC-BT.1359-1-199811-I!!PDF-E.pdf"},{"claim":"Apple documents AVAudioSession.outputLatency as the latency for audio output in seconds, and says an AirPlay device can add a 2-second delay.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/avfaudio/avaudiosession/outputlatency"},{"claim":"The Web Audio look-ahead metronome example wakes a scheduler every 25 ms and schedules sounds due in the next 100 ms on the audio clock.","asOf":"2026-10-05","src":"https://web.dev/articles/audio-scheduling"}]],["Transferable skills by discipline","#/map/t/careers-transferable-skills",[{"claim":"Farhan Noor’s crowd-sourced tracker, which logs publicly reported layoffs and estimates where no number is given, recorded about 8,500 game industry layoffs in 2022, 10,500 in 2023 and 14,600 in 2024, as reported by Game Developer. The counts are a floor, not a census.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/industry-layoffs-are-seemingly-slowing-but-the-damage-has-already-been-done"},{"claim":"GDC’s 2026 State of the Game Industry survey (more than 2,300 respondents) found 28 percent of respondents had been laid off in the previous two years, and 33 percent of those based in the United States.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gdconf.com/article/gdc-2026-state-of-the-game-industry-reveals-impact-of-layoffs-generative-ai-and-more/"},{"claim":"The same GDC 2026 survey found 36 percent of respondents use generative AI at work, mostly for research and brainstorming (81 percent), code assistance (47 percent) and prototyping (35 percent). 52 percent said it is having a negative impact on the industry (30 percent in 2025, 18 percent in 2024) and 7 percent a positive one (13 percent in 2025). Visual and technical art (64 percent), game design and narrative (63 percent) and programming (59 percent) held the most negative views. Sentiment is self-reported.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://gdconf.com/article/gdc-2026-state-of-the-game-industry-reveals-impact-of-layoffs-generative-ai-and-more/"}]],["Where game developers go","#/map/t/careers-destinations",[{"claim":"ILM states that over 50 percent of The Mandalorian season one was filmed in StageCraft, a 20 ft high, 270-degree LED volume with a 75 ft performance space, driven in real time by Unreal Engine.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.ilm.com/groundbreaking-led-stage-production-technology-created-for-hit-lucasfilm-series-the-mandalorian/"},{"claim":"For season two, ILM enlarged the StageCraft volume, raised its resolution and introduced Helios, a real-time render engine built by ILM engineers, for the imagery shown on the LED walls.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.ilm.com/vfx/the-mandalorian-season-2/"},{"claim":"Epic says the GMC Hummer EV became, in 2020, the first vehicle to feature Unreal Engine in its digital cockpit, as a proof of concept, and that over 35 car models have since shipped with Unreal Engine HMI (as of Epic’s five-year article, not re-checked for 2026).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.unrealengine.com/news/five-years-35-car-models-how-unreal-engine-is-accelerating-hmi-innovation"},{"claim":"Epic describes the Rivian R1T as the first commercial vehicle to ship to the mass market with an Unreal Engine powered digital cockpit.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.unrealengine.com/news/five-years-35-car-models-how-unreal-engine-is-accelerating-hmi-innovation"},{"claim":"In a Unity-hosted talk from November 2023, Mercedes-Benz’s director of MB.OS customer experience and design explains why the company selected Unity for its in-car experiences. That is a 2023 statement of intent; check whether it still holds before citing it as current.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/resources/unite-mercedes-benz"},{"claim":"BMW Group says its Omniverse-based Virtual Factory covers more than 30 sites and projects up to a 30 percent reduction in production planning costs.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.press.bmwgroup.com/global/article/detail/T0450699EN/bmw-group-scales-virtual-factory?language=en"},{"claim":"Unity lists 3D collaboration, immersive training, customer experiences and embedded HMI as Unity Industry uses, and says its Asset Transformer toolkit prepares data from 70+ file formats, including CAD and BIM.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/products/unity-industry"}]],["Repositioning your portfolio and CV","#/map/t/careers-repositioning",[{"claim":"Ladders’ 2018 eye-tracking study of recruiters found an average initial CV screen of 7.4 seconds; simple layouts with clear headings and bulleted accomplishments performed best.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ladders-updates-popular-recruiter-eye-tracking-study-with-new-key-insights-on-how-job-seekers-can-improve-their-resumes-300744217.html"},{"claim":"Nielsen Norman Group, drawing on a survey of 204 UX hiring managers, recommends three to five detailed case studies, each covering the problem, your role, the process, the outcome and the business impact, and notes hiring managers rarely read a portfolio word for word.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ux-design-portfolios/"}]],["Interviewing outside games","#/map/t/careers-interviewing-outside-games",[{"claim":"Amazon’s interview loop page says candidates meet several interviewers individually, that questions are behavioural and tied to its Leadership Principles, and it recommends the STAR method.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.amazon.jobs/content/en/how-we-hire/interview-loop"}]],["The AI tooling market as a destination","#/map/t/careers-ai-tooling-market",[{"claim":"The Stanford HAI AI Index 2026 reports global private AI investment of $344.7 billion in 2025, up 127.5% from 2024, with United States investment at $285.9 billion.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://hai.stanford.edu/news/inside-the-ai-index-12-takeaways-from-the-2026-report"},{"claim":"The Stanford HAI AI Index 2026 reports that private investment in generative AI grew more than 200% in 2025 and took nearly half of all private AI funding.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/2026-ai-index-report/economy"}]],["\"Or just be a farmer\", answered","#/map/t/careers-the-farmer-answered",[{"claim":"USDA ERS estimates that in 2024 the median farm household had farm income of -$1,830, off-farm income of $86,900 and total household income of $102,748.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.ers.usda.gov/topics/farm-economy/farm-household-well-being/farm-household-income-estimates"},{"claim":"USDA ERS reports that in 2024 residence (small) farm households had median farm income of -$2,748 and median total household income of $120,848, and that 35 percent had positive farm income.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.ers.usda.gov/topics/farm-economy/farm-household-well-being/farm-household-income-estimates"},{"claim":"GIANTS Software announced on 18 November 2025 that Farming Simulator 25 had sold 4 million copies since its launch on 12 November 2024, and that the series had sold more than 40 million.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.giants-software.com/news.php?show=news-134"}]],["Coding interviews for game roles","#/map/t/careers-game-coding-interviews",[{"claim":"Robert Nystrom’s Game Programming Patterns is free to read online and covers patterns that come up in game interviews, including Object Pool, Spatial Partition, Game Loop and Component.","asOf":"2026-09-30","src":"https://gameprogrammingpatterns.com/"}]],["Steam (platform guide)","#/platforms/steam",[{"claim":"The Steam Direct fee is $100 per game, paid back to you once the game earns $1,000 in adjusted gross revenue.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/appfee"},{"claim":"A new partner waits 30 days after paying the app fee before the first game can release; tax verification can take 2 to 7 business days.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/onboarding"},{"claim":"Steam Deck Verified checks controller input and on-screen glyphs, default settings that run well, no launcher warnings, and text at least 9 pixels tall at 1280 by 800.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/steamdeck/compat"},{"claim":"SteamPipe uploads are scripted: steamcmd runs +login <build account> +run_app_build <app_build VDF> +quit, and the app build script names the AppID, ContentRoot, BuildOutput and one depot build script per depot.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/sdk/uploading"},{"claim":"GameCI’s game-ci/steam-deploy@v3 GitHub Action uploads from CI with the secrets STEAM_USERNAME, STEAM_CONFIG_VDF (a base64 config.vdf from a local Steam Guard login) and STEAM_APP_ID, plus rootPath, depot paths and a releaseBranch.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://game.ci/docs/github/deployment/steam"},{"claim":"Steam Cloud comes two ways: Auto-Cloud syncs configured folders with no code, and the ISteamRemoteStorage API gives the game control; each game sets a byte quota and a file count per user.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/cloud"},{"claim":"Store page review and build review each typically take 3 to 5 business days; Valve asks for submission at least 7 business days before launch.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/store/review_process"},{"claim":"US tax on US-source income defaults to 30% withholding for non-US partners, reduced where a tax treaty applies.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/finance/taxfaq"},{"claim":"A new game must be public as Coming Soon for at least two weeks before it can release.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/store/coming_soon"},{"claim":"Payouts are monthly, once a month’s sales pass $100, about 30 days after the month ends.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/finance/payments_salesreporting"},{"claim":"Valve’s revenue share was reported in 2018 as 30% up to $10 million per game, 25% to $50 million, and 20% above; the agreement itself is not public.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://gameinformer.com/2018/11/30/valve-adjusting-revenue-share-for-steams-most-popular-games"},{"claim":"Players can refund within 14 days of purchase if they have played under 2 hours.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://store.steampowered.com/steam_refunds/"},{"claim":"Steam Next Fest runs three times a year; a game needs a public demo and must be unreleased, and can take part only once.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/marketing/upcoming_events/nextfest"},{"claim":"SteamPipe splits each file into roughly 1 MB chunks and sends only changed chunks, so Valve advises limiting pack files to about 1 or 2 GB, grouping assets by level, not reordering them, and aligning Unreal paks to 1 MB.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/sdk/uploading"},{"claim":"A build can be set live on a beta branch automatically with SetLive in the app build script, but never on the default branch: that is done by hand on the Builds page, and setting a build live on a released app is confirmed by the account’s phone number or Steam Mobile App.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/sdk/uploading"}]],["Nintendo Switch (platform guide)","#/platforms/nintendo",[{"claim":"The Nintendo Developer Portal accepts individual developers; access to Switch information needs a separate application after registration.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.nintendo.com/the-process"},{"claim":"The portal states that Nintendo is not currently accepting requests for access to the Switch 2 development environment.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.nintendo.com/web/development/home/developing-for-switch2"},{"claim":"The Godot project states that console export needs third-party middleware or a porting company, because console SDKs are under NDA.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://godotengine.org/consoles/"},{"claim":"IARC lists Nintendo among its storefronts.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://globalratings.com/storefronts/"},{"claim":"CERO (Japan) is not among IARC’s rating authorities.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://globalratings.com/participants/"}]],["PlayStation (platform guide)","#/platforms/playstation",[{"claim":"PlayStation Partners registration asks where the applicant’s company or institution is registered.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://register.playstation.net/"},{"claim":"In 2022 Sony offered newly registered partners with an accepted concept one dev kit and one test kit on loan; check with your partner contact that this still applies.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://sonyinteractive.com/en/news/blog/complimentary-development-hardware/"},{"claim":"Unreal Engine’s PS5 support is available after you confirm approved PlayStation 5 developer status with Epic.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/development-for-playstation-5-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"IARC lists PlayStation among its storefronts.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://globalratings.com/storefronts/"},{"claim":"Sony states that once registration is approved and the Global Developer and Publisher Agreement (GDPA) is signed, partners get development tools, publishing resources, documentation and Help Center support.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://sonyinteractive.com/en/news/blog/showing-your-game-to-playstation/"}]],["Xbox and Microsoft Store (platform guide)","#/platforms/xbox",[{"claim":"ID@Xbox requires members to be 18 or older, to sign an NDA and to be in a country where Microsoft can do business.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.microsoft.com/en-us/games/publish/"},{"claim":"The Microsoft Store developer registration no longer charges a fee under the new onboarding.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/get-started"},{"claim":"Xbox Requirements version 16.4 is dated 8 September 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/store/policies/console/certification-requirements"},{"claim":"Console games must ship with 10 to 100 achievements worth 1,000 gamerscore at launch, and must treat Series X and Series S equally.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/store/policies/console/certification-requirements"},{"claim":"Secure GDK downloads need an organisation onboarded as an Xbox partner; personal Microsoft accounts are no longer supported.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/gdk-dev/development-downloads/access-resources"},{"claim":"Certification targets 4 business days for a base digital game, 2 for a content update and 5 for a disc submission.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/game-publishing/concepts/certification/certification-guide"},{"claim":"Microsoft Store policy 10.8.4 requires the odds of each item in a paid random reward to be shown before purchase.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/store-policies"},{"claim":"The Microsoft Store (PC) fee for games sold through Microsoft’s commerce is 12%.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/get-started"},{"claim":"Microsoft’s publishing guide says PC games built with the GDK use the MSIXVC package format and console games use XVC, both made with MakePkg.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/game-publishing/tutorial-xbox-managed/how-to-create-a-package"},{"claim":"XR-055 caps a single achievement at 200 gamerscore and requires every achievement to be achievable; XR-057 requires base-game achievements to be earnable without buying in-title content and forbids changing an achievement’s unlock rules once it is published.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/store/policies/console/certification-requirements"},{"claim":"The Xbox Accessibility Guidelines are version 3.2, published 8 June 2023, and Microsoft describes them as best practices rather than a compliance checklist.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/accessibility/guidelines"},{"claim":"Partner Center’s Packages page accepts .msix, .msixupload, .msixbundle, .appx, .appxupload and .appxbundle files, and recommends .msixupload or .appxupload for Windows 10 and later.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/publish-your-app/msix/upload-app-packages"},{"claim":"An update to a published MSIX app can roll out gradually to a percentage of Windows 10 or 11 customers, and the percentage can be raised or the update halted without a new submission.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/publish-your-app/msix/upload-app-packages"},{"claim":"An MSIX submission has Pricing and availability, Properties, Age ratings, Packages, Store listings and Submission options pages, then Submit for certification; the Microsoft Store submission API can automate it.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/publish-your-app/msix/create-app-submission"}]],["Google Play (platform guide)","#/platforms/google-play",[{"claim":"Play Console registration costs $25 once.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/6112435"},{"claim":"Personal accounts created after 13 November 2023 need a closed test with at least 12 testers opted in for 14 days in a row before applying for production.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/14151465"},{"claim":"Android developer verification is enforced on certified devices from 30 September 2026 in Brazil, Indonesia, Singapore and Thailand, and worldwide from 2027.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.android.com/developer-verification"},{"claim":"From 31 August 2026 new apps and updates must target Android 16 (API 36); an extension to 1 November 2026 can be requested.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/11926878"},{"claim":"Android vitals flags a game when user-perceived crashes pass 1.09% or freezes (ANRs) pass 0.47%, averaged over 28 days.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.android.com/games/optimize/vitals"},{"claim":"New apps are enrolled in Play App Signing automatically: you sign the bundle with an upload key (RSA 2048-bit or more) and Google signs the APKs it delivers with the app signing key it manages.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9842756"},{"claim":"Gradle Play Publisher uploads with ./gradlew publishBundle and moves a release with ./gradlew promoteArtifact; track defaults to internal, userFraction to 0.1, and releaseStatus to COMPLETED, with credentials from a service account JSON key. Its maintainers now accept pull requests but ignore issues.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/Triple-T/gradle-play-publisher"},{"claim":"fastlane supply (upload_to_play_store) takes --aab, --track, --rollout and --json_key, and its track_promote_to option promotes a release to another track.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.fastlane.tools/actions/upload_to_play_store/"},{"claim":"Google says review can take up to seven days, or longer in exceptional cases, for some developer accounts.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859751"},{"claim":"The Data safety form is required for every app on closed, open or production tracks, including apps that collect no data.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/10787469"},{"claim":"Google announced a new service-fee model on 4 March 2026, taking effect by region between 30 June 2026 and 30 September 2027; until then the rest of the world pays 15% on the first $1 million a year and 30% above.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/112622"},{"claim":"Pre-registration campaigns can run for up to 90 days.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9084187"},{"claim":"Internal testing allows up to 100 testers; closed testing lists hold up to 2,000 users each, with up to 50 lists per track; open testing is unlimited unless you set a cap of at least 1,000.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9845334"},{"claim":"Staged rollouts apply only to updates, not a first release; percentages do not increase automatically, a halt stops new users getting the update, and countries cannot be removed once a rollout starts.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/6346149"}]],["Apple App Store (platform guide)","#/platforms/apple",[{"claim":"The Apple Developer Program costs $99 per membership year, with fee waivers for eligible nonprofits, schools and government bodies.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/programs/whats-included/"},{"claim":"Organisations enrol with a legal entity, a D-U-N-S number, legal binding authority and a public website.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/programs/enroll/"},{"claim":"Since 28 April 2026 apps must be built with Xcode 26 and the iOS 26 SDK or later.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/news/upcoming-requirements/"},{"claim":"TestFlight supports up to 100 internal and 10,000 external testers; external builds need beta review.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/testflight/"},{"claim":"A TestFlight build can be tested for up to 90 days; the first build added to an external group must pass TestFlight App Review, and later builds may not need a full review.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/test-a-beta-version/testflight-overview"},{"claim":"fastlane match (sync_code_signing) syncs certificates and profiles across a team, pilot (upload_to_testflight) uploads builds to TestFlight, and deliver (upload_to_app_store) uploads metadata and the binary to App Store Connect.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.fastlane.tools/actions/"},{"claim":"Apple states that on average 90% of submissions are reviewed in less than 24 hours.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/distribute/app-review/"},{"claim":"Since iOS 14.5, apps need App Tracking Transparency permission to track users across other companies’ apps and websites or to read the IDFA, which returns zeros without it; privacy labels list data types, use for tracking and linkage to identity.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developer.apple.com/app-store/user-privacy-and-data-use/"},{"claim":"Apple’s age ratings are 4+, 9+, 13+, 16+ and 18+; developers had to answer the new rating questions by 31 January 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/news/?id=ks775ehf"},{"claim":"Guideline 3.1.1 requires apps with loot boxes to disclose the odds of each item type before purchase.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/"},{"claim":"Members of Apple’s Small Business Program (no more than $1 million in proceeds in the prior calendar year, or new to the App Store; enrolment required) pay 15% on paid apps and in-app purchases, instead of the standard 30%.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/app-store/small-business-program/"},{"claim":"Since 1 May 2025, apps on the US storefront may link to outside purchase methods.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/news/?id=9txfddzf"},{"claim":"In the EU, from 1 October 2026, App Store sales through Apple In-App Purchase pay 26% (15% for small-business and similar programmes), alternative in-app payment processing 20% (10%), and linked out-of-app offers a 15% store services commission (10%) on sales within 7 days of the tap.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developer.apple.com/support/dma-and-apps-in-the-eu/"},{"claim":"In the EU, apps distributed through alternative marketplaces or Web Distribution pay a 5% Core Technology Commission from 1 October 2026, replacing the per-install Core Technology Fee; the initial acquisition fee is removed.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developer.apple.com/support/dma-and-apps-in-the-eu/"},{"claim":"Phased release goes 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%, 50% and 100% of users who have automatic updates on, over seven days, and can be paused for up to 30 days in total.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/update-your-app/release-a-version-update-in-phases"}]],["Epic Games Store (platform guide)","#/platforms/epic",[{"claim":"Epic charges a recoupable $100 submission fee per game.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://store.epicgames.com/en-US/distribution"},{"claim":"Only members with the Owner or Admin role can create a product in the Developer Portal; products, sandboxes and deployments are managed on the Product Settings page.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/docs/dev-portal/product-management"},{"claim":"The BuildPatch Tool (1.8.8, the recommended version) uploads binaries to a product’s Dev sandbox with -mode=UploadBinary, taking the organisation, product and artifact IDs, the BPT client ID and secret, a BuildRoot, a BuildVersion unique per artifact whatever the platform, an AppLaunch executable path and an optional CloudDir cache; other modes list, copy, diff, delete and delta-optimise binaries.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/docs/epic-games-store/publishing-tools/uploading-binaries/bpt-instructions-180"},{"claim":"BPT uses separate BuildPatch Tool credentials from the BPT credentials tab of Product Settings; clients listed under SDK Credentials cannot be used with it, and the secret is passed as ClientSecret or, as Epic recommends, ClientSecretEnvVar.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/docs/epic-games-store/publishing-tools/uploading-binaries/bpt-instructions-180"},{"claim":"An artifact update marked Bugfix can be published without review, straight from the Dev to the Live sandbox, only to fix broken functionality; adding, removing or changing a platform needs a new review.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/docs/epic-games-store/publishing-tools/publishing-process/patch-live-builds"},{"claim":"Promoting an artifact from Dev to Stage or Live does not promote its deployment ID, which must be set again in the destination sandbox.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/docs/epic-games-store/store-presence/manage-artifacts"},{"claim":"Since June 2025 developers keep 100% of the first $1 million of net revenue per product each year (the allowance resets on 1 January), then 88%, on purchases made with Epic’s payments.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://store.epicgames.com/en-US/news/epic-games-store-updates-revenue-share-keep-100-of-the-first-1m-per-product-per-year"},{"claim":"Epic announced in May 2025 Epic-hosted webshops for out-of-app purchases, with a 0% store fee on the first $1,000,000 per app per year and 88/12 after that on payments Epic processes, and 5% Epic Rewards for players.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://store.epicgames.com/en-US/news/new-epic-games-store-webshops-and-revenue-share-update"},{"claim":"Each product can have one web shop; enabling it needs a product with early store discovery, a working webhook and the Epic Games Store Web Shop Agreement, which only an owner or signatory role can sign.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/docs/epic-games-store/store-presence/web-shops/manage-web-shops"},{"claim":"Artifact update types include Bugfix for small updates that do not affect the age rating; patch notes are required for major updates only.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/docs/epic-games-store/store-presence/manage-artifacts"}]],["Web and browser portals (platform guide)","#/platforms/web",[{"claim":"Access to Poki for Developers is by request: developers share details about themselves and Poki reaches out to grant access.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.poki.com/share"},{"claim":"itch.io lets anyone create a game jam instantly with a free account.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/game-jams.md"},{"claim":"CrazyGames limits a game to a 50 MB initial download, 250 MB in total and 1,500 files; the mobile homepage needs an initial download of 20 MB or less.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.crazygames.com/requirements/technical/"},{"claim":"CrazyGames’ Basic Launch needs no SDK and has no monetisation, and ends after at least 7 days and 500 plays, or after 21 days; progression to Full Launch depends on average playtime, conversion to gameplay and retention, benchmarked against other games. A game with some metrics at benchmark may be offered another Basic Launch period; one with most below must be resubmitted as a new game with significant improvements.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.crazygames.com/"},{"claim":"Poki gives developers 100% of revenue from players who reach the game directly and splits 50/50 for players who come through Poki.com or Poki marketing, and asks for exclusivity on the open web only.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://sdk.poki.com/"},{"claim":"CrazyGames’ main monetisation is an advertising revenue share, and only ads served through its SDK are allowed.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.crazygames.com/requirements/intro/"}]],["Meta Quest (platform guide)","#/platforms/quest",[{"claim":"An organisation must verify an admin’s identity or the business before it can publish or update apps; admin verification with a government ID usually takes minutes.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/resources/publish-organization-verification/"},{"claim":"Apps created after 1 March 2026 must target Android 14 (API 34).","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/blog/meta-quest-apps-android-14-march-1/"},{"claim":"Immersive apps must keep rendering at 60 fps or more at an allowed refresh rate.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/resources/vrc-quest-performance-1/"},{"claim":"Immersive apps must run 45 minutes without crashing or freezing.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/resources/vrc-quest-functional-1/"},{"claim":"Meta asks developers to submit for review at least two weeks ahead of launch.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/resources/publish-submit/"},{"claim":"Age-group self-certification is required for every app on each submission or update: teens and adults, mixed ages, or children.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/resources/age-groups/"},{"claim":"Meta keeps 30% of net revenue; for subscriptions its share drops to 15% from the fourth month.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/policy/developer-distribution-agreement/"},{"claim":"On 19 February 2026 Meta said it is separating Quest VR from Worlds, shifting Worlds to be almost exclusively mobile, removing individual worlds from VR store shelves and separating them from the Store in its mobile app; it reported that third-party apps take 86% of time spent in headsets.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/blog/2026-vr-state-of-the-union-horizon-mobile-focus/"},{"claim":"After launch, builds uploaded to production update immediately without review, and metadata changes usually take 1 to 2 business days.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/resources/publish-after-sub/"},{"claim":"From 5 August 2024 Meta moved App Lab content onto the Meta Horizon Store under one set of VRCs, and apps could opt into an Early Access badge.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/blog/get-apps-ready-app-lab-meta-horizon-store-meta-quest-developers/"},{"claim":"Meta announced in April 2024 that App Lab would no longer be a separate distribution channel and that every title meeting basic technical, content and privacy requirements would be published.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/blog/a-more-open-ecosystem-for-developers/"},{"claim":"The default release channels are Production, RC, BETA and ALPHA; a channel holds 200 users by default (up to 2,500 on request), testers join by email, alias or URL, and only Production is reviewed.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/resources/publish-release-channels/"},{"claim":"ovr-platform-util upload-quest-build needs --app-id, --app-secret or --token, --apk, --channel and --age-group (without it the upload is left as a draft), with optional --obb, --notes, --assets-dir and --debug-symbols-dir; channel names are not case-sensitive and store means Production.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.meta.com/horizon/resources/publish-reference-platform-command-line-utility/"}]],["itch.io (platform guide)","#/platforms/itch",[{"claim":"New accounts have a soft limit of 20 project pages and should keep to 10 files per page; itch.io raises the limits by request.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/faq.md"},{"claim":"Indexing needs a Public page with no Public access limits, a cover image, and a project that can be bought, downloaded or played in the browser; empty or placeholder pages are not indexed, though devlogs on a public page are.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/getting-indexed.md"},{"claim":"butler push <dir> <user>/<game>:<channel> uploads a build; channel names containing win, linux, mac or android tag the platform, --userversion or --userversion-file sets the version, and after the first push only new data is uploaded, typically saving 80 to 95%.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/butler/master/docs/pushing.md"},{"claim":"An HTML5 ZIP must contain an index.html, and by default no more than 1,000 files, 500 MB extracted in total, 200 MB for any single file, and paths of at most 240 characters; itch.io can lift the limits on request.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/html5.md"},{"claim":"A new seller’s first paid project is queued for review before indexing, generally within a few business days; the page stays published and works by URL, and established sellers with no history of issues are indexed immediately.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/getting-indexed.md"},{"claim":"itch.io treats mass-produced automatically generated or AI-generated pages as spam that may be removed from browse and search, and asks that a project using AI generation state it clearly in its description.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/quality-guidelines.md"},{"claim":"Pages with strong adult themes must be marked under Metadata, Classification, and itch.io may remove all of a creator’s pages from search and browse if they consistently ignore it.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/quality-guidelines.md"},{"claim":"itch.io does not allow pornographic images or videos of real-life humans; fictional, illustrated and rendered content is generally fine if legal, and sellers must follow the acceptable use policies of their payment processors.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/faq.md"},{"claim":"itch.io’s open revenue sharing, launched in 2015, lets sellers set itch.io’s share, with 10% as the default.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/itch-io-launches-open-revenue-sharing"},{"claim":"The Open Revenue Share slider in account settings sets itch.io’s cut from 0% to 100%, 10% by default.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/payments.md"},{"claim":"On itch.io any price is a minimum price: buyers may pay more, and free content still offers the option to pay before download.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/pricing.md"},{"claim":"After a push the default patch is available right away, and an optimised patch is regenerated on the server, which can take around 30 minutes for large games.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/butler/master/docs/pushing.md"}]],["Roblox (platform guide)","#/platforms/roblox",[{"claim":"Accounts are split into Roblox Kids (5 to 8), Roblox Select (9 to 15) and Roblox (16 and over); a game runs a trial for age-checked players 16 and over and qualifies for under-16s at 250 unique plays by highly engaged age-checked players within 60 days.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/publishing/kids-and-select"},{"claim":"The all-ages publishing fee is 1,000 Robux per game, refunded once the game keeps 25 highly engaged players for 60 days without moderation; expedited review costs 50,000 Robux for a 48-hour review, and either fee is forfeited if the game is permanently moderated.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/publishing/publish-games-and-places"},{"claim":"Creating a group costs 100 Robux.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/projects/groups"},{"claim":"Server Authority mode (Workspace.AuthorityMode = Server) makes the server the single source of truth: clients send inputs, predict locally, and roll back when they mispredict.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/projects/server-authority"},{"claim":"A DataStore value can hold up to 4,194,304 characters per key, and each server’s default budget is 60 + 40 × players standard reads a minute, and the same for writes.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/cloud-services/data-stores/error-codes-and-limits"},{"claim":"MessagingService messages are at most 1 kB, and delivery is best effort, not guaranteed.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/MessagingService"},{"claim":"Android is about 65% of a typical game’s players, and about 60% of those devices have 2 to 4 GB of RAM.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/performance-optimization/test-on-hardware"},{"claim":"Luau type checking has three modes, set on a script’s first line: --!nocheck, --!nonstrict (the default) and --!strict.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/luau/type-checking"},{"claim":"Studio’s Server & Clients test mode simulates up to 8 clients.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/studio/testing-modes"},{"claim":"Team Create live scripting auto-saves edits every 5 minutes; Drafts mode is off by default, and switching it needs every collaborator to leave the session.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/projects/collaboration"},{"claim":"The Open Cloud Place Publishing API is a POST to apis.roblox.com/universes/v1/{universeId}/places/{placeId}/versions?versionType=Published with an .rbxl or .rbxlx file, and needs an API key with universe-places write access to that game; it does not update EditableImage, EditableMesh, PartOperation, SurfaceAppearance or BaseWrap instances, which must be published from Studio.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/cloud/guides/usage-place-publishing"},{"claim":"Rojo is an open-source community tool, not maintained by Roblox; rojo build -o build.rbxl turns a project folder into a place file.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://rojo.space/docs/v7/getting-started/new-game/"},{"claim":"Creator Store sellers price models and plugins in US dollars and keep 100% of net proceeds.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/creator-store"},{"claim":"Minimal and Mild games can be seen on every account tier, Moderate games by Select and 16-and-over accounts, and Restricted games only by age-verified players 18 and over.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/promotion/content-maturity"},{"claim":"The questionnaire is under Creator Dashboard, the game, Configure, Questionnaire; a preview page shows the resulting maturity label, descriptors and regional compliance issues before Submit, and a Restricted label restarts servers to remove players who are not age-verified.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/promotion/content-maturity"},{"claim":"Paid random item odds must be shown as percentages summing to exactly 100% before purchase, including when the item is bought with a currency or key that was bought with Robux.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/monetization/paid-random-items"},{"claim":"Passes pay the creator 70% of the Robux spent.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/monetize-experiences"},{"claim":"An avatar item bought inside a game pays 30% to the item’s creator and 40% to the game’s owner.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/marketplace/marketplace-fees-and-commissions"},{"claim":"Roblox Plus subscribers get 10% off, rising to 20% from the third month, paid by Roblox rather than the creator; a game earns 250 Robux a month for three months for each new subscriber it signs up.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/monetization/roblox-plus"},{"claim":"Creator Rewards pay 5 Robux a day for each Active Spender (someone who spent at least $9.99 in the past 60 days) for whom the game is one of their first three of the day and who plays it at least 10 minutes.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/creator-rewards"},{"claim":"DevEx pays $0.0038 per Earned Robux ($114 per 30,000) for Robux earned from 2025-09-05; older balances cash out at $0.0035 and must be cashed out first. It needs age 13 or over, at least 30,000 Earned Robux, a verified email, a DevEx Portal account and a W-9 or W-8 tax form.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/monetization/developer-exchange"},{"claim":"From 2026-06-08, a higher DevEx rate of $0.0054 applies to Robux from developer products, passes, subscriptions and private servers bought by US players verified 18 and over, in games where no player character is ever R6.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/monetization/18-plus-devex-rate"},{"claim":"A place file can be at most 100 MB, and at most 5 never-public games can be made public per day.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/publishing/publish-games-and-places"},{"claim":"A game can show up to 10 thumbnail images or videos at 16:9; videos must show real gameplay, and only 3 video uploads are allowed a month.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/publishing/thumbnails"},{"claim":"An in-game experiment runs for 14 to 60 days with a control and up to two variants.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/experiments"},{"claim":"Roblox names play-through rate, first-play bounce rate, play days per player and playtime per player (capped at 60 minutes a day) as its most important ranking signals.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/discovery"},{"claim":"Ads Manager campaigns aim at Plays, Earnings or Engagement, and Robux converted to ad credits cannot be converted back.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/promotion/ads-manager"},{"claim":"A game’s social media links are shown only to age-verified players 16 and over, and only such creators can add them.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/promotion/social-media-links"}]],["Fortnite (UEFN) (platform guide)","#/platforms/fortnite",[{"claim":"The Fortnite Developer Program needs age 18 or over, a real-money purchase through Epic Payments or at least $20 spent in Fortnite in the last 365 days, and a creator code of 3 to 16 characters; payouts also need a tax interview and a Hyperwallet account.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/fortnite/fortnite-developer-program"},{"claim":"Nobody can collaborate on a UEFN project with anyone under 18.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/fortnite/collaborating-in-unreal-editor-for-fortnite"},{"claim":"Scene Graph is still labelled Beta, and Epic advises caution when shipping with it.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/fortnite/scene-graph-in-unreal-editor-for-fortnite"},{"claim":"UEFN runs on PC only, while Fortnite Creative runs on Switch, Xbox, PlayStation, Android, iOS and PC.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/fortnite/install-and-launch-fortnite-creative-and-unreal-editor-for-fortnite"},{"claim":"Epic’s Lore Version Control page (its old Unreal Revision Control address now redirects there) calls Lore an open-source version control tool maintained by Epic that powers UEFN’s in-editor version control, saving the island and its assets as incremental revisions; the Check-in Changes window asks for a changelist description.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/fortnite/lore-version-control-in-unreal-editor-for-fortnite"},{"claim":"Fortnite caps island ratings at Teen (ESRB) in North America and PEGI 12 in Europe; an island rated above a region’s cap is unavailable there, and one above the cap everywhere cannot be published.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/fortnite/iarc-overview-and-faqs-in-fortnite-creative"},{"claim":"The Engagement Pool receives 40% of eligible net revenue from the Item Shop and related real-money purchases, plus 50% of Sponsored Row revenue (100% from that feature’s launch through the end of 2026); engagement is measured per calendar month and paid 30 days after the month ends.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/fortnite/engagement-payout-in-fortnite-creative"},{"claim":"Creators receive 50% of the V-Bucks value of in-island sales, raised to 100% from 2026-01-09 through 2027-01-31; V-Bucks value is real-money spend minus platform fees.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/fortnite/in-island-transactions-overview-in-fortnite"},{"claim":"In-island items cost 50 to 5,000 V-Bucks in steps of 50, work only in the island where they were bought, and cannot be gifted to another player.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/fortnite/in-island-transactions-overview-in-fortnite"},{"claim":"Every island is enrolled in automatic A/B thumbnail testing, which creators can opt out of.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/fortnite/publishing-islands-in-fortnite-creative"},{"claim":"A release moves through six Creator Portal screens: Game Details (including what changed from the previous release), Rating (an IARC contact email and questionnaire), In-Island Transactions, Promotional Media, Attribution for third-party assets, and Visibility (publish automatically, on a scheduled date and time, or manually later, as Listed or Unlisted); a red exclamation mark flags any screen missing information.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/fortnite/publishing-from-the-creator-portal-in-fortnite-creative"},{"claim":"New islands are tested in Discover for up to two weeks.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/fortnite/how-discover-works-in-fortnite"},{"claim":"Discover measures average playtime (capped at 120 minutes a session), concurrent players, bounce rate, retention, social play, qualified play-through rate and 7-day unique players.","asOf":"2026-09-24","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/fortnite/how-discover-works-in-fortnite"}]],["Godot (engine guide)","#/engines/godot",[{"claim":"The current stable release is Godot 4.7.2, released on 18 August 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://godotengine.org/download/windows/"},{"claim":"Web export supports only the Compatibility renderer (WebGL 2), and Godot 4 C# projects cannot be exported to the web.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/export/exporting_for_web.html"},{"claim":"Threaded web builds need SharedArrayBuffer, which needs the Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin and Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp headers, or the PWA service-worker workaround.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/export/exporting_for_web.html"},{"claim":"iOS export must be done on macOS with Xcode installed, using an Apple developer Team ID and a unique bundle identifier.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/export/exporting_for_ios.html"},{"claim":"The Godot Foundation maintains no official console ports; W4 Games offers Switch, Xbox Series and PlayStation 5 ports, and vendors such as RAWRLAB, Lone Wolf Technology and Pineapple Works also port.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://godotengine.org/consoles/"},{"claim":"Godot is released under the MIT (Expat) licence, owes no royalties, and asks games to include its copyright and licence notice; the Godot Foundation hosts the project.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://godotengine.org/license/"},{"claim":"The renderers are Forward+ (Vulkan, Direct3D 12 or Metal, desktop), Mobile (same APIs, mobile and desktop) and Compatibility (OpenGL, low-end devices and the web).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/rendering/renderers.html"}]],["Unity (engine guide)","#/engines/unity",[{"claim":"Unity 6.6 (6000.6) was released on 1 September 2026 as a Supported release, the last before Unity 6.7 LTS.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://discussions.unity.com/t/unity-6-6-is-now-available/1735357"},{"claim":"Unity 6.3 is the latest LTS, supported until December 2027; Unity 6.0 LTS is supported through October 2026; LTS releases get two years, plus one more for Enterprise and Industry.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/releases/unity-6/support"},{"claim":"A .meta file holds the asset unique ID and all its import settings; moving an asset outside Unity without its .meta breaks references.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.6/Documentation/Manual/AssetMetadata.html"},{"claim":"Asset Serialization Mode offers Mixed, Force Binary and Force Text, and Force Text is the default.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.6/Documentation/Manual/class-EditorManager.html"},{"claim":"IL2CPP is an ahead-of-time pipeline that converts C# IL to C++ and compiles it to native code, and is required on several platforms where JIT is not available.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.6/Documentation/Manual/scripting-backends.html"},{"claim":"Build Profiles, opened from File, Build Profiles, save multiple independent build configurations per platform.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.6/Documentation/Manual/build-profiles.html"},{"claim":"Building for PlayStation, Xbox and Nintendo platforms needs an active Unity Pro subscription or a Preferred Platform licence key from the platform holder, plus developer approval from each platform holder.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/solutions/console"},{"claim":"The Built-In Render Pipeline is deprecated and will be made obsolete in a future release; it is supported through the Unity 6.7 LTS lifecycle.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.6/Documentation/Manual/render-pipelines.html"},{"claim":"Personal is free up to $200,000 revenue and funding; Pro is $2,310 per seat per year or $210 per month, required above $200,000; Enterprise is required above $25 million with custom pricing.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/products/pricing-updates"},{"claim":"On 12 September 2024 CEO Matt Bromberg cancelled the Runtime Fee for games, effective immediately, and raised Pro by 8% and Enterprise by 25% from 1 January 2025.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/blog/unity-is-canceling-the-runtime-fee"},{"claim":"The per-install fee announced in September 2023 was due from 1 January 2024; the 22 September 2023 open letter exempted Personal, limited it to the next LTS shipping in 2024 onward, exempted games under $1 million in revenue, and let studios pay the lesser of 2.5% of revenue or the install-based fee.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://unity.com/blog/an-open-letter-to-our-community"}]],["GameMaker (engine guide)","#/engines/gamemaker",[{"claim":"The default release is LTS 2026.0.0 (May 2026); the Beta channel is at 2026.100 (from July 2026), and the Monthly 2024 series was superseded by LTS 2026.0.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://releases.gamemaker.io/"},{"claim":"The LTS build has a two-year lifecycle and receives only critical bug and security fixes; Beta receives fixes and features sooner.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/download"},{"claim":"Hotline Miami by Dennaton Games was developed with GameMaker.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/blog/hotline-miami-developed-with-gamemaker"},{"claim":"Undertale by Toby Fox has a page in the official GameMaker showcase.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/showcase/undertale"},{"claim":"Hyper Light Drifter and Chicory: A Colorful Tale are listed on the official GameMaker showcase page.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/showcase"},{"claim":"Free exports to GX.games, desktop, web and mobile for non-commercial use; Professional adds a commercial licence without console; Enterprise adds console exports and source code access.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/get"},{"claim":"Enterprise console targets are Nintendo Switch, PlayStation 4, PlayStation 5, Xbox One and Xbox Series X|S; mobile targets include iOS, Android, Amazon Fire, Android TV and tvOS (export overview of June 2023).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/blog/export-with-gamemaker"},{"claim":"Console access requires joining the platform program (Nintendo developer, PlayStation Partners or ID@Xbox) and a monthly or yearly Enterprise subscription.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/help/articles/application-process-for-console-access"},{"claim":"YYC compiles to machine code, which runs faster but takes longer to compile, and needs platform SDKs configured in Preferences.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://manual.gamemaker.io/monthly/en/Settings/YoYo_Compiler.htm"},{"claim":"On 21 November 2023 GameMaker became free for non-commercial use on all non-console platforms, with a one-time commercial licence replacing the subscriptions.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/blog/gamemaker-free-platforms"},{"claim":"The Professional licence costs $99.99 as a one-time purchase, with regional pricing, and console export needs Enterprise.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/help/articles/november-2023-pricing-terms-change-faq"},{"claim":"The GameMaker price page lists Enterprise at $79.99 monthly or $799.99 yearly in US dollars; the same page shows the Professional price in the visitor’s local currency.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://gamemaker.io/en/get"},{"claim":"Opera acquired YoYo Games, maker of GameMaker, for about $10 million on 20 January 2021 and formed Opera Gaming.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://press.opera.com/2021/01/20/opera-buys-yoyo-games-gamemaker-opera-gaming/"},{"claim":"Igor builds, runs and packages GameMaker projects from the command line for desktop, web, mobile and console targets, and can drive continuous integration through Jenkins or GitHub Actions; it needs a licence file from a prior IDE login.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://manual.gamemaker.io/lts/en/Settings/Building_via_Command_Line.htm"}]],["Unreal Engine (engine guide)","#/engines/unreal",[{"claim":"Unreal Engine 5.8 was released on 17 June 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://forums.unrealengine.com/t/unreal-engine-5-8-released/2729274"},{"claim":"Hotfix 5.8.3 was released on 22 September 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://forums.unrealengine.com/t/5-8-3-hotfix-released/2833315"},{"claim":"Unreal Engine 5.8 marks MegaLights, Movie Render Graph and, for licensees, the Iris replication system as production ready, and adds Lumen Lite, a medium-quality global illumination setting that targets 60fps on PlayStation 5.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/unreal-engine-5-8-release-notes"},{"claim":"Epic’s Perforce guide states that .uasset files are binary and cannot be merged in a text-based tool, and that the editor checks out files so others cannot edit them concurrently.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/using-perforce-as-source-control-for-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"The DDC is not committed to version control, can be regenerated from the .uasset files, and uses the Unreal Zen Store as the local DDC by default from UE 5.4.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/using-derived-data-cache-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"One File Per Actor stores actor data in external files and is enabled automatically with World Partition.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/one-file-per-actor-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"BuildCookRun runs build, cook, stage, package, deploy and run stages, started with RunUAT from Engine/Build/BatchFiles.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/build-operations-cooking-packaging-deploying-and-running-projects-in-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"UE 5.8 lists Android Studio Koala 2024.1.2 Patch 1, NDK r27c, build-tools 35.0.1, OpenJDK 21.0.3, a recommended target SDK of 35 and a minimum install SDK of 26; Google Play has required target API 35 since 31 August 2025, which needs UE 5.7 or newer.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/android-development-requirements-for-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"BuildGraph is an XML script-based build automation system of nodes, agents and triggers that integrates with UnrealBuildTool and AutomationTool and can distribute work across a farm.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/buildgraph-for-unreal-engine"},{"claim":"Console resources are free but gated: the company must already be an approved Xbox, PlayStation or Switch developer, submit Epic’s console access request form and sign a Console NDA, and the platform extension files then come through the Epic Developer Portal, not GitHub.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/community/learning/knowledge-base/B9xk/getting-started-with-console-development-for-unreal-engine-and-epic-online-services"},{"claim":"A 5% royalty applies to lifetime gross revenue above $1 million directly attributable to the Unreal product, regardless of who collects it; Epic Games Store sales are royalty-free.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.unrealengine.com/license"},{"claim":"Epic’s 2024 pricing set seats at $1,850 per seat per year, with regional pricing, for companies over $1 million in annual gross revenue that are not creating games; games and runtime apps licensed to end users pay royalties instead.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.unrealengine.com/en-US/blog/we-are-updating-unreal-engine-twinmotion-and-realitycapture-pricing-in-late-april"},{"claim":"The Launch Everywhere with Epic programme gives a preferred royalty rate of 3.5% instead of the standard 5%, chosen on the product’s Release Form.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://dev.epicgames.com/docs/dev-portal/unreal-engine/release-forms-and-royalties/release-notifications"}]],["Web stack (engine guide)","#/engines/web",[{"claim":"caniuse lists WebGPU as on by default in Chrome and Edge 113+, Opera 99+, Safari on iOS 26+ and Samsung Internet 24+, partial in desktop Safari 26 and off by default in Firefox, reaching about 87% of global users.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://caniuse.com/webgpu"},{"claim":"MDN’s compatibility data records WebGPU in Chrome for Android from 121 and in Firefox from 141 as a partial implementation: on Windows since 141 and on Apple-silicon macOS since 145, but not on Linux, Intel Macs or Android.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/GPU"},{"claim":"Poki pays the developer 100% of revenue from players who arrive directly, through bookmarks, search, social media or the developer’s own community, and splits Poki-sourced revenue 50/50; it requires web exclusivity, which does not cover Steam, app stores or consoles.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.poki.com/guide/working-with-poki"},{"claim":"CrazyGames allows a total size of 250 MB and an initial download of 50 MB or less, 20 MB or less to qualify for the mobile homepage, and at most 1500 files.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.crazygames.com/requirements/technical/"},{"claim":"itch.io runs an HTML5 upload in an iframe: a multi-file game must be a ZIP containing index.html, file names are case sensitive, paths must be relative because the game sits in a subdirectory of the HTML CDN, and Embed options offer Embed in page with a viewport size or Click to launch in fullscreen.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/itchio/itch.io/master/docs/creators/html5.md"},{"claim":"CrazyGames disables monetisation (video ads, banners and in-game purchases) during Basic Launch, a test with a limited audience lasting 7 to 21 days; revenue share starts at Full Launch.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.crazygames.com/"},{"claim":"three.js is released under the MIT License.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/dev/LICENSE"},{"claim":"Phaser is released under the MIT License, copyright Richard Davey and Phaser Studio Inc.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/phaserjs/phaser/blob/master/LICENSE.md"},{"claim":"Babylon.js is released under the Apache License 2.0.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/BabylonJS/Babylon.js/blob/master/license.md"},{"claim":"The PlayCanvas engine is released under the MIT License, copyright PlayCanvas Ltd.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/playcanvas/engine/blob/main/LICENSE"}]],["Ren’Py (engine guide)","#/engines/renpy",[{"claim":"The current release is Ren’Py 8.5.3, released 15 May 2026; the SDK develops games for Windows, Mac and Linux, with Android and iOS tools available separately.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/latest.html"},{"claim":"Ren’Py’s own site names the desktop version of Doki Doki Literature Club as a Ren’Py game.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/"},{"claim":"Serenity Forge’s Zhenghua Yang says Doki Doki Literature Club Plus! was ported to Unity, begun by Unity Technologies’ programmers and finished by Serenity Forge with Team Salvato engineers.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://mcvuk.com/business-news/insisting-that-ddlc-must-be-experienced-in-one-specific-way-would-be-a-very-elitist-and-pretentious-mindset-for-us-to-have-behind-the-scenes-of-doki-doki-literature-club-plus/"},{"claim":"RPG Maker MZ, released in summer 2020, builds games from a database of actors, skills and enemies plus menu-driven events, extended with JavaScript plugins.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.rpgmakerweb.com/products/rpg-maker-mz"},{"claim":"Labels, jump and call are documented as the core control flow of the script language.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/label.html"},{"claim":"Ren’Py documents layered images for composing sprites from attribute layers.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/layeredimage.html"},{"claim":"The translate command creates or updates translation files in tl/<language>, and extract_strings and merge_strings move existing translations to and from JSON.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/cli.html"},{"claim":"Saving, loading and rollback are documented together, including which state is saved and how rollback interacts with Python.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/save_load_rollback.html"},{"claim":"The launcher builds a PC zip for Windows and Linux, a Linux tar.bz2, a Mac app zip for Intel and Apple Silicon, a Windows zip, and a Markets package for itch.io and Steam.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/build.html"},{"claim":"Steam support is installed through the launcher, is distributed only to creators accepted into the Steam partner program, and is configured with config.steam_appid.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/achievement.html"},{"claim":"Android builds need JDK 21 and produce a Play Bundle for Google Play or a universal APK, both capped at 2 GB; the keystores must be kept to ship updates.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/android.html"},{"claim":"iOS apps can only be created on a Mac with Xcode and membership of the Apple developer program.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/ios.html"},{"claim":"The web platform is in beta; it cannot make arbitrary network requests or use threads, and audio waits for a player interaction.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/web.html"},{"claim":"Most of Ren’Py is MIT-licensed; portions derive from LGPL code, so games must be distributed in a way that satisfies the LGPL.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/license.html"},{"claim":"The command line offers lint, which checks the script for errors and prints statistics, plus compile, test, distribute, android_build and web_build.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/cli.html"},{"claim":"Ren’Py 8.0 runs under Python 3 while 7.5 continued Python 2.7, and Ren’Py 8.4 moved to Python 3.12 on all platforms.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/changelog.html"}]],["Defold (engine guide)","#/engines/defold",[{"claim":"Latest stable release is Defold 1.13.1 (17 August 2026); 1.13.2 beta and 1.14.0 alpha appeared on 10 September 2026.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/defold/defold/releases"},{"claim":"Defold was acquired by King in 2014, released free at GDC in March 2016, and its source was released and moved to the Defold Foundation in May 2020.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/about/"},{"claim":"The Defold showcase lists Void Scrappers by 8BitSkull (Q4 2022) on Steam and Nintendo Switch, and Interrogation by Critique Gaming, published by Mixtvision (2019), on Switch, Windows, Linux, macOS, Android and iOS.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://defold.com/showcase/"},{"claim":"On the vendor’s own table, an empty Defold 1.13.0 project with default release settings is a 0.83 MB compressed HTML5 (Wasm) download, against 11.73 MB for Unity 6.5 and 6.68 MB for Godot 4.7.1; the table header says Brotli and its footnote says gzip.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/product/"},{"claim":"msg.post queues messages for asynchronous delivery to on_message, addressed as socket:/path#fragment, with a 2 KB limit on the message table and no functions allowed.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/manuals/message-passing/"},{"claim":"Nintendo Switch support is not in the standard version; registered Nintendo developers sign up for Defold on the Tools and Middleware page and receive the extension source, command-line bundling and a forum.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/manuals/nintendo-switch/"},{"claim":"Licensed PS4 and PS5 developers confirm their status on DevNet Tools and Middleware to get Defold console support, with bundling from the command line only.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/manuals/sony-playstation/"},{"claim":"Steam and Facebook Instant Games are supported through extensions.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/product/"},{"claim":"Defold does not yet support Microsoft Xbox; the Defold Foundation is working towards becoming an Xbox middleware provider and asks approved Xbox developers to request Defold support from their Xbox developer contact.","asOf":"2026-09-29","src":"https://defold.com/manuals/microsoft-xbox/"},{"claim":"The Defold License is derived from Apache 2.0: games may be commercialised, the editor and engine may not, and the licence and copyright notice must be included.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/license/"},{"claim":"Defold describes its source as available rather than Open Source: the OSI definition requires that the source can be commercialised, and the Defold Foundation chose to prevent commercialisation of the engine and editor.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/open/"},{"claim":"bob.jar resolves, builds and bundles Defold projects for desktop, iOS, Android and HTML5 from the command line and needs OpenJDK 25.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://defold.com/manuals/bob/"}]],["Cocos Creator (engine guide)","#/engines/cocos",[{"claim":"The latest Cocos Creator download is 3.8.8, released 16 December 2025.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.cocos.com/en/creator-download"},{"claim":"On 4 January 2026 SUD, which owns Cocos, announced COCOS 4 in a press release as fully open source under MIT, separating the engine from the editor and claiming over 1.7 million developers.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/cocos-4-is-here-fully-open-source-302652264.html"},{"claim":"The cocos4 repository is MIT-licensed, with a C++ core and TypeScript user API, targeting Windows and Android on Vulkan, macOS and iOS on Metal, and web on WebGL; its latest release is 4.0.0-alpha.34 (20 September 2026), and there is no stable 4.0.0 tag.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/cocos/cocos4/releases"},{"claim":"Publish targets listed are iOS, Android, Huawei AppGallery Connect, HarmonyOS, Google Play, Google Play on Games, macOS, Windows, Web Mobile, Web Desktop, Facebook Instant Games, WeChat, Douyin, OPPO, vivo and Honor mini games, and Huawei Quick Games.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.cocos.com/creator/manual/en/editor/publish/"},{"claim":"The WeChat mini game package cannot exceed 4 MB including code and assets; subpackages are supported, the engine plugin is js-only, and the WebGL 2 option is experimental and forced off by default; separately, debugging WebAssembly needs base library 2.12.0 or later.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.cocos.com/creator/manual/en/editor/publish/publish-wechatgame.html"},{"claim":"The cocos-pnp plugin, maintained by an individual developer rather than Cocos, packages a Cocos 2.4 or 3.x web build into playable ads for networks including AppLovin, Facebook, Google and ironSource.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/ppgee/cocos-pnp"},{"claim":"SUD’s COCOS 4 press release says it adopts the MIT licence and removes all commercial-related clauses originally included in Cocos Creator.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/cocos-4-is-here-fully-open-source-302652264.html"},{"claim":"The cocos-engine repository’s LICENSE.md, created in December 2022, is the MIT licence, and the v3.8.8 tag carries it.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/cocos/cocos-engine/blob/v3.8.8/LICENSE.md"},{"claim":"Command-line builds use --project and --build with platform or configPath parameters; exit code 36 means success, 32 invalid parameters, 34 a build failure.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.cocos.com/creator/manual/en/editor/publish/publish-in-command-line.html"}]],["Blender (engine guide)","#/engines/blender",[{"claim":"The latest release tag is Blender 5.2.2, dated 14 September 2026; the 4.5 line is still receiving patch releases (4.5.14).","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://api.github.com/repos/blender/blender/tags"},{"claim":"-b or --background runs Blender without its interface, with audio disabled by default; -P or --python runs a script file; --python-exit-code sets an exit code from 0 to 255 when a Python exception is raised, and 0 turns it off.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/blender/blender/blob/v5.2.2/source/creator/creator_args.cc"},{"claim":"Godot imports .blend files directly by running Blender’s glTF export behind the scenes; it needs Blender 3.0 or later (3.5 or later recommended), with the path set under Filesystem, Import, Blender, Blender Path.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_3d_scenes/available_formats.html"},{"claim":"The glTF exporter offers GLB and glTF Separate, exports +Y up by default, and leaves Apply Modifiers off by default because it prevents exporting shape keys.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/blender/blender/blob/v5.2.2/scripts/addons_core/io_scene_gltf2/__init__.py"},{"claim":"Blender itself is released under the GNU General Public License 3.0 or later, © 2011-2026 Blender Foundation, and it bundles dependencies under other licences.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/blender/blender/blob/v5.2.2/release/license/license.md"},{"claim":"The Blender readme states it can be used freely for any purpose, including commercial use and distribution.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/blender/blender/blob/v5.2.2/release/text/readme.html"},{"claim":"GPL 3 section 2: output from running a covered work is covered by the licence only if that output, given its content, is itself a covered work.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/blender/blender/blob/v5.2.2/doc/license/GPL3-license.txt"},{"claim":"The UPBGE manual, which carries over Blender’s game-licensing text, says the software and source code are bound to the GNU GPL but blend-files (models, textures, sounds) are not.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://upbge.org/docs/latest/manual/manual/deployment/licensing.html"},{"claim":"mcp-for-blender is a third-party add-on and MCP server; its execute_blender_code tool runs arbitrary Python in Blender, its README says to always save your work before using it, and its socket server has no authentication or encryption.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://github.com/ahujasid/mcp-for-blender"},{"claim":"Claude’s Blender connector is made by Blender Lab and gives a natural-language interface to Blender’s Python API; it needs an add-on inside Blender.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://claude.com/connectors/blender"}]],["Curated and regional channels","#/platforms",[{"claim":"Apple Arcade games play without ads or in-app purchases.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developer.apple.com/apple-arcade/"},{"claim":"Each mini game version is reviewed before it can be released, normally within one calendar day.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.weixin.qq.com/minigame/introduction/guide/bbsh.html"},{"claim":"The main package is capped at 4 MB and all packages together at 30 MB.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://developers.weixin.qq.com/minigame/dev/guide/base-ability/subPackage/useSubPackage.html"},{"claim":"GOG reviews every submission, contacts the developer only if it will release the game, and asks for games that are finished or already released elsewhere.","asOf":"2026-09-28","src":"https://support.gog.com/hc/en-us/articles/11382878039197-Releasing-your-game-on-GOG-FAQ"}]]],"20d348f7");
